var tafsirData_11 = {
  1: {
    "Alif-Lãm-Ra.": "These are some of the disconnected letters that appear at the beginning of certain chapters in the Quran. Their exact meaning is known only to Allah. They serve to capture attention and demonstrate that the Quran, though composed of letters known to the Arabs, is miraculous and beyond human ability to replicate.",
    "˹This is˺ a Book whose verses are well perfected": "This Quran is a Book whose verses are firmly established, precise, and free from any contradiction or flaw. Every word is placed with divine wisdom, and no error or inconsistency can be found in it. Unlike human writings that contain mistakes, this Book is perfect in its construction.",
    "and then fully explained.": "After being perfected, its meanings have been made clear and detailed. Allah has explained everything people need for guidance—what is lawful, what is forbidden, stories of the past, and news of the future. Nothing essential has been left out.",
    "˹It is˺ from the One ˹Who is˺ All-Wise, All-Aware.": "This Book comes from Allah, Who possesses complete wisdom in all He decrees and does, and Who has full knowledge of all things—nothing is hidden from Him. Because it comes from such a source, it can be trusted completely."
  },
  2: {
    "˹Tell them, O Prophet,˺ \"Worship none but Allah.": "The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) is instructed to convey the most fundamental message: direct all worship exclusively to Allah. This means all prayers, sacrifices, supplications, love, fear, and hope should be for Allah alone, with no partners or intermediaries. This was the core message of every prophet sent by Allah.",
    "Surely I am a warner": "The Prophet's role includes warning people about the consequences of disbelief and disobedience—the punishment of Hellfire and Allah's displeasure. This warning is an act of mercy, giving people the chance to change before it's too late.",
    "and deliverer of good news to you from Him.": "Alongside the warning, the Prophet brings glad tidings of Paradise, Allah's mercy, and eternal rewards for those who believe and do righteous deeds. Islam is balanced—it inspires both hope and caution."
  },
  3: {
    "And seek your Lord's forgiveness": "Ask Allah to pardon your sins and shortcomings. Seeking forgiveness is essential because all humans make mistakes. Allah loves those who turn to Him in repentance, as the Prophet (peace be upon him) said he sought Allah's forgiveness more than seventy times a day.",
    "and turn to Him in repentance.": "Repentance means more than just asking for forgiveness—it means sincerely regretting the sin, stopping it immediately, and resolving never to return to it. True repentance involves a complete turning back to Allah with one's heart and actions.",
    "He will grant you a good provision for an appointed term": "As a reward for sincere repentance, Allah promises a comfortable and blessed life in this world until the time of death. This doesn't necessarily mean wealth, but contentment, peace of mind, and sufficiency in one's needs.",
    "and graciously reward the doers of good.": "Those who go beyond the basics and excel in good deeds will receive extra rewards from Allah's generosity. Allah never lets any good deed go unrewarded, and He multiplies the reward for those who are excellent in their worship and dealings with others.",
    "But if you turn away, then I truly fear for you the torment of a formidable Day.": "If people reject this message and refuse to repent, the Prophet expresses genuine concern for their fate on the Day of Judgment—a Day that will be tremendous and terrifying for the disbelievers. This shows the Prophet's compassion even for those who rejected him."
  },
  4: {
    "To Allah is your return.": "Every single person will return to Allah after death for judgment. There is no escape from this meeting. This reality should motivate people to prepare for that Day by living righteously.",
    "And He is Most Capable of everything.": "Allah has complete power over all things—including bringing the dead back to life, rewarding the righteous, and punishing the wicked. Nothing is difficult for Him, so the resurrection that some doubt is easy for the One who created everything from nothing."
  },
  5: {
    "Indeed, they enfold ˹what is in˺ their hearts, ˹trying˺ to hide it from Him!": "Some people try to conceal their disbelief, hatred, or evil intentions from Allah by hiding their true feelings deep within themselves. They think that if they don't express their thoughts openly, Allah won't know. This is a foolish assumption.",
    "But even when they cover themselves with their clothes, He knows what they conceal and what they reveal.": "Even if they wrap themselves completely in their garments, trying to hide physically and emotionally, Allah still knows everything. Covering oneself with clothes cannot hide one's inner thoughts from the Creator. Allah's knowledge penetrates all barriers.",
    "Surely He knows best what is ˹hidden˺ in the heart.": "Allah has perfect and complete knowledge of what lies in every heart—every secret intention, hidden grudge, concealed doubt, and private thought. Nothing escapes His awareness. This should make a person mindful of their inner state, not just their outward actions."
  },
  6: {
    "There is no moving creature on earth whose provision is not guaranteed by Allah.": "Every living thing that moves on the earth—whether human, animal, insect, or any other creature—has its sustenance guaranteed by Allah. He takes responsibility for feeding all of His creation. This should remove excessive worry about provision and build trust in Allah.",
    "And He knows where it lives and where it is laid to rest.": "Allah knows the dwelling place of every creature—where it lives, sleeps, and takes shelter—and He knows where it will die and be buried. Nothing about any creature's existence is unknown to Him.",
    "All is ˹written˺ in a perfect Record.": "Every detail about every creature is recorded in the Preserved Tablet (Al-Lawh Al-Mahfuz). This comprehensive record contains everything that was, is, and will be. Allah's knowledge is so complete that it is all documented perfectly."
  },
  7: {
    "He is the One Who created the heavens and the earth in six Days": "Allah created the entire universe—the heavens and the earth and everything in them—in six days. This demonstrates His perfect power and wisdom. He could have created everything instantly, but He chose to create in stages to teach us patience and deliberate action.",
    "—and His Throne was upon the waters—": "Before the creation of the heavens and earth, Allah's Throne was above water. This describes the state before creation began. The Throne is the greatest of Allah's creations, and it is above all of creation.",
    "in order to test which of you is best in deeds.": "The purpose of creation is to test humanity—not to see who does the most deeds, but who does the best deeds. Quality matters more than quantity. The best deeds are those done sincerely for Allah and in accordance with the Prophet's guidance.",
    "And if you ˹O Prophet˺ say, \"Surely you will ˹all˺ be raised up after death,\"": "When the Prophet informs people about resurrection—that they will be brought back to life after death for judgment—the disbelievers reject this fundamental truth.",
    "the disbelievers will certainly say, \"That is nothing but pure magic!\"": "Instead of accepting the truth, the disbelievers dismiss the concept of resurrection as magic or trickery. They refuse to believe because resurrection contradicts their desires to live without accountability. Their denial doesn't change the reality of what's to come."
  },
  8: {
    "And if We delay their punishment until an appointed time,": "Allah, in His wisdom, may delay the punishment of the disbelievers to a specific time that He has decreed. This delay is not because He is unable, but because everything happens according to His perfect timing.",
    "they will definitely say, \"What is holding it back?\"": "Because the punishment doesn't come immediately, the disbelievers mockingly ask what's delaying it. They use the delay as supposed proof that there is no punishment coming. This mockery is a sign of their arrogance and ignorance.",
    "Indeed, on the Day it overtakes them, it will not be averted from them,": "When the punishment finally arrives, there will be no escape, no delay, and no way to push it back. It will be decisive and final. All their mockery will be meaningless then.",
    "and they will be overwhelmed by what they used to ridicule.": "The very thing they mocked and denied will surround them completely. They will be engulfed by the punishment they thought would never come. Their ridicule will turn into regret."
  },
  9: {
    "If We give people a taste of Our mercy": "When Allah blesses people with good things—health, wealth, security, or any form of comfort—it is a taste of His mercy and favor upon them.",
    "then take it away from them,": "If Allah, in His wisdom, removes that blessing as a test or for reasons known to Him—perhaps to purify them or redirect their focus—many people respond poorly.",
    "they become utterly desperate, ungrateful.": "Instead of being patient and trusting in Allah's wisdom, they fall into despair and hopelessness. They also become ungrateful, forgetting all the previous blessings they enjoyed and focusing only on what they lost. This reveals a weak faith and lack of trust in Allah."
  },
  10: {
    "But if We give them a taste of prosperity after being touched with adversity,": "On the other hand, when Allah relieves their hardship and blesses them with good times after they experienced difficulties, another negative reaction emerges.",
    "they say, \"My ills are gone,\"": "They declare that their troubles have departed, often attributing the change to their own efforts or luck rather than acknowledging Allah's mercy and favor.",
    "and become totally prideful and boastful,": "Instead of being grateful to Allah and humble, they become arrogant about their good fortune. They boast to others and feel superior, forgetting that everything they have is from Allah and can be taken away just as easily."
  },
  11: {
    "except those who patiently endure and do good.": "The exception to these negative responses are the believers who maintain patience during hardship and continue doing good deeds regardless of their circumstances. They don't despair in difficulty or become arrogant in prosperity. They remain steady and grateful.",
    "It is they who will have forgiveness and a mighty reward.": "These patient, righteous people will receive Allah's forgiveness for their sins and a tremendous reward in the Hereafter. Their steadfastness and good character will be rewarded beyond measure."
  },
  12: {
    "Perhaps you ˹O Prophet˺ may wish to give up some of what is revealed to you": "Allah addresses the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), acknowledging that the constant rejection and unreasonable demands of the disbelievers might make him feel like abandoning some parts of his message that they find particularly objectionable.",
    "and may be distressed by it": "The Prophet might feel tightness in his chest and emotional pain because of their stubborn refusal to believe and their hurtful words.",
    "because they say, \"If only a treasure had been sent down to him, or an angel had come with him!\"": "The disbelievers demanded physical miracles according to their whims—they wanted the Prophet to have wealth as proof or to be accompanied by a visible angel. These demands showed they weren't genuinely seeking truth but looking for excuses to reject.",
    "You are only a warner,": "Allah reminds the Prophet of his role—he is a warner sent to deliver the message, not to perform miracles on demand or force people to believe. His job is to convey, and guidance is in Allah's hands.",
    "and Allah is the Trustee of All Affairs.": "Allah is in control of everything. The Prophet should put his trust in Allah and not be overwhelmed by the disbelievers' behavior. Allah will handle all matters and bring everything to its proper conclusion."
  },
  13: {
    "Or do they say, \"He has fabricated this ˹Quran˺!\"?": "The disbelievers accused the Prophet of inventing the Quran himself, claiming it was not from Allah but from his own imagination. This was a serious accusation that Allah addresses directly.",
    "Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"Produce ten fabricated sûrahs like it": "Allah challenges them: if you think a human made this up, then you produce just ten chapters like it. Since you claim it's fabricated, you should be able to fabricate something similar. This challenge has never been met in over 1400 years.",
    "and seek help from whoever you can—other than Allah—": "They are told to gather all their poets, scholars, experts, and anyone else they can find to help them in this task. They can even call upon their false gods for assistance.",
    "if what you say is true!\"": "If their claim that the Quran is man-made is true, then this challenge should be easy for them. The fact that they cannot meet it proves the Quran's divine origin."
  },
  14: {
    "But if your helpers fail you,": "When all those they call upon for help cannot produce anything like the Quran—and they never will—this failure is itself proof.",
    "then know that it has been revealed with the knowledge of Allah,": "Their inability to meet the challenge proves that the Quran was revealed with Allah's knowledge and is not a human creation. It contains knowledge that no human could possess.",
    "and that there is no god ˹worthy of worship˺ except Him!": "This also proves the fundamental truth of Islam—that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah alone. The miracle of the Quran testifies to His oneness.",
    "Will you ˹not˺ then submit ˹to Allah˺?": "Given this clear proof, the logical response is to submit to Allah in Islam. This is an invitation to accept the truth and become Muslim."
  },
  15: {
    "Whoever desires ˹only˺ this worldly life and its luxuries,": "Those whose sole goal and intention is this temporary worldly life with its pleasures, decorations, and comforts—with no concern for the Hereafter—have made a terrible trade.",
    "We will pay them in full for their deeds in this life": "Allah will give them the results of their worldly efforts in this life. They may achieve wealth, fame, pleasure, or whatever they were working for.",
    "—nothing will be left out.": "They will receive their worldly reward completely, without any reduction. Allah is just and gives everyone what they worked for in this world."
  },
  16: {
    "It is they who will have nothing in the Hereafter except the Fire.": "The tragic consequence of living only for this world is that in the Hereafter, they will have nothing but the punishment of Hell. They already received their payment in this life.",
    "Their efforts in this life will be fruitless": "All their worldly accomplishments will be worthless in the Hereafter. Their hard work will bring them no benefit when they need it most.",
    "and their deeds will be useless.": "Everything they did will be nullified because it was done without faith and without seeking Allah's pleasure. Deeds without proper intention and belief have no value with Allah."
  },
  17: {
    "˹Can these people be compared to˺ those ˹believers˺ who stand on clear proof from their Lord,": "True believers are not following blind faith—they stand upon clear evidence and guidance from Allah. They have certainty based on knowledge and proof.",
    "backed by ˹the Quran as˺ a witness from Him,": "The Quran itself serves as a witness confirming the truth of their faith. It provides ongoing testimony and evidence.",
    "and preceded by the Book of Moses ˹which was revealed˺ as a guide and mercy?": "Before the Quran, there was the Torah given to Moses, which also served as guidance and mercy for its people. The previous scriptures confirm and support the message of the Quran.",
    "It is those ˹believers˺ who have faith in it.": "These people—who have clear proof, the witness of the Quran, and confirmation from previous scriptures—truly believe in the message.",
    "But whoever from the ˹disbelieving˺ groups rejects it, the Fire will be their destiny.": "Anyone from any group or faction who rejects this clear truth after it has come to them will have the Hellfire as their promised destination. This applies to all who deny, regardless of their background.",
    "So do not be in doubt of it.": "This is a command to have complete certainty and no hesitation about the truth of the Quran and its message.",
    "It is certainly the truth from your Lord,": "The Quran is definitely, without any doubt, the absolute truth that has come from your Lord.",
    "but most people do not believe.": "Despite the clear proofs, the majority of people refuse to believe. This is a sad reality, but it should not shake the faith of the believers or discourage the Prophet."
  },
  18: {
    "Who does more wrong than those who fabricate lies against Allah?": "There is no one more unjust and sinful than those who invent falsehoods and attribute them to Allah. This includes those who claim Allah has partners, those who forge false scriptures, and those who make up religious rulings without authority.",
    "They will be brought before their Lord,": "On the Day of Judgment, these liars will be made to stand before Allah to face their reckoning. There will be no escape.",
    "and the witnesses will say, \"These are the ones who lied against their Lord.\"": "The witnesses—which may include angels, prophets, or even their own body parts—will testify against them, publicly exposing and humiliating them by identifying them as liars against their Lord.",
    "Surely Allah's condemnation is upon the wrongdoers,": "Allah's curse and removal from His mercy is upon all those who commit such grave injustice. Being cursed by Allah is the worst possible fate."
  },
  19: {
    "who hinder ˹others˺ from Allah's Path,": "These wrongdoers actively prevent people from following the straight path of Islam. They create obstacles, spread doubts, and discourage others from accepting the truth.",
    "striving to make it ˹appear˺ crooked,": "They try to make the clear, straight path of Allah seem crooked, confusing, or unappealing. They distort the truth and misrepresent Islam to turn people away.",
    "and disbelieve in the Hereafter.": "On top of their other crimes, they deny the Day of Judgment entirely. They reject accountability and live as if there are no consequences for their actions."
  },
  20: {
    "They will never frustrate Allah on earth,": "These wrongdoers cannot escape Allah's grasp or outsmart His plan while on earth. No matter how powerful or clever they think they are, they are completely under Allah's control.",
    "and they will have no protector besides Allah.": "On the Day of Judgment, they will find no one to protect them or intercede for them. All the false allies and supporters they had in this world will abandon them.",
    "Their punishment will be multiplied,": "Because they not only disbelieved themselves but also led others astray, their punishment will be increased. They will bear the burden of their own sins plus the sins of those they misled.",
    "for they failed to hear or see ˹the truth˺.": "They could not truly hear the message of truth or see the clear signs because they willfully rejected guidance. Their failure was not due to inability but due to stubbornness and arrogance."
  },
  21: {
    "It is they who have ruined themselves,": "These people have brought destruction upon themselves through their own choices. They have lost their souls and their chance at eternal happiness because of their disbelief and misdeeds.",
    "and whatever ˹gods˺ they fabricated will fail them.": "All the false gods and partners they invented and associated with Allah will completely disappear and be of no use to them. Their fabricated beliefs will provide no help or benefit."
  },
  22: {
    "Without a doubt, they will be the worst losers in the Hereafter.": "There is absolutely no doubt that these people will suffer the greatest loss on the Day of Judgment. While others may have lost things in this world, these people have lost everything—their souls, their eternity, and any chance of happiness forever. They are the ultimate losers."
  },
  23: {
    "Surely those who believe,": "Those who have true faith in their hearts—believing in Allah, His messengers, His books, His angels, the Last Day, and divine decree.",
    "do good,": "Their belief is accompanied by righteous actions. True faith always produces good deeds. They worship Allah, treat people well, and fulfill their obligations.",
    "and humble themselves before their Lord": "They approach Allah with humility, submission, and a soft heart. They are not arrogant or self-righteous but recognize their dependence on Allah and their own weaknesses.",
    "will be the residents of Paradise.": "These humble believers will be the inhabitants of Paradise—the eternal garden of bliss prepared by Allah for His faithful servants.",
    "They will be there forever.": "Their stay in Paradise is not temporary. They will remain there eternally, enjoying Allah's pleasure and rewards without end, never to be removed or to experience death again."
  },
  24: {
    "The example of these two parties is that of the blind and the deaf,": "The disbelievers are like people who are blind and deaf—they cannot see the truth that is clearly visible, and they cannot hear the guidance that is being called out to them. Their hearts are sealed to guidance.",
    "compared to the seeing and the hearing.": "The believers, in contrast, are like people with perfect sight and hearing—they see the signs of Allah all around them and hear the message of truth clearly. They respond and follow guidance.",
    "Can the two be equal?": "This is a rhetorical question with an obvious answer: absolutely not! There is no comparison between someone who can see and hear versus someone who is blind and deaf. Similarly, the believer and disbeliever are in completely different positions.",
    "Will you not then be mindful?": "This is a call to reflection and contemplation. People should think about this comparison and take heed. They should choose to be among those who see and hear the truth, not among those who are spiritually blind and deaf. The choice is clear for anyone who reflects."
  },
  25: {
    "Surely We sent Noah to his people.": "Allah sent Prophet Noah (peace be upon him) to his people as a messenger. Noah was one of the first major messengers, sent to guide his people who had fallen into idol worship. His mission lasted an incredibly long time—950 years of calling people to the truth.",
    "˹He said,˺ \"Indeed, I am sent to you with a clear warning": "Noah's message was straightforward and unmistakable. He came as a clear warner, leaving no room for confusion about what he was calling them to and what the consequences of rejection would be."
  },
  26: {
    "that you should worship none but Allah.": "The core message of Noah, like all prophets, was pure monotheism—to worship Allah alone without any partners. This is the fundamental message of Islam that every prophet brought to their people.",
    "I truly fear for you the torment of a painful Day.\"": "Noah expressed genuine concern for his people. His warning came from a place of love and care—he feared they would face a severely painful punishment if they continued in their disbelief. This shows the compassion prophets have for those they are sent to guide."
  },
  27: {
    "The disbelieving chiefs of his people said,": "The leaders and elite among Noah's people, who were deeply entrenched in disbelief, responded to his message. These were the wealthy and powerful who had the most to lose if people accepted Noah's call to equality before Allah.",
    "\"We see you only as a human being like ourselves,": "Their first objection was that Noah was just a human. They expected a messenger from Allah to be something extraordinary—perhaps an angel. This same objection was raised against Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) centuries later. They failed to understand that Allah sends human messengers so people can relate to them and follow their example.",
    "and we see that no one follows you except the lowliest among us,": "They looked down upon Noah's followers because they were poor, weak, and of low social status. The arrogant chiefs considered this a mark against Noah's message, when in reality, the humble and oppressed are often the first to recognize and accept the truth because they have no worldly status to protect.",
    "who do so ˹hastily˺ without thinking.": "They accused the believers of accepting faith without proper thought or deliberation—implying they were simple-minded. In truth, the believers recognized the truth immediately because their hearts were pure and uncorrupted by arrogance.",
    "We do not see anything that makes ˹all of˺ you any better than us.": "The chiefs saw no superiority in Noah or his followers in terms of wealth, power, or social standing. They measured worth by worldly standards, not by faith and righteousness.",
    "In fact, we think you are liars.\"": "They concluded by accusing Noah and his followers of lying. This baseless accusation was their way of dismissing the message without genuinely engaging with its truth."
  },
  28: {
    "He said, \"O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord": "Noah invites them to reflect: what if he truly has clear evidence and certainty from Allah? He is asking them to consider the possibility that he is telling the truth, even if they cannot see it.",
    "and He has blessed me with a mercy from Himself,": "Allah has bestowed upon Noah the blessing of prophethood and guidance—a tremendous mercy. This gift of revelation and understanding is from Allah's grace.",
    "which you fail to see.": "This mercy and clear proof has been made invisible to them because of their own arrogance and rejection. Their hearts are veiled, so they cannot perceive what Noah has been given.",
    "Should we ˹then˺ force it on you against your will?": "Noah clarifies that faith cannot be forced. He cannot compel them to believe if they are determined to reject. Guidance is from Allah, and each person must choose to accept it. This teaches an important principle: there is no compulsion in religion."
  },
  29: {
    "O my people! I do not ask you for a payment for this ˹message˺.": "Noah makes clear he has no worldly motive. He is not seeking wealth, payment, or personal gain from his mission. This removes any suspicion that he might be a con artist or motivated by greed.",
    "My reward is only from Allah.": "His compensation comes solely from Allah. This is the way of all true prophets—they seek their reward from Allah alone, not from people.",
    "And I will never dismiss the believers,": "Noah refuses to expel the poor and humble believers to please the arrogant chiefs. Some leaders demanded he remove the \"lowly\" followers before they would consider listening to him. Noah firmly refuses because faith makes all believers equal.",
    "for they will surely meet their Lord.": "These believers will stand before Allah on the Day of Judgment. How could Noah wrong them by dismissing them when they will face their Lord and he will be accountable for how he treated them?",
    "But I can see that you are a people acting ignorantly.": "Noah observes that their demands and behavior stem from ignorance. They do not understand the reality of faith, the equality of believers before Allah, or the consequences of their arrogance."
  },
  30: {
    "O my people! Who would protect me from Allah if I were to dismiss them?": "Noah asks a powerful rhetorical question: if he were to wrong the believers by expelling them, who would save him from Allah's punishment? No one can protect anyone from Allah's justice.",
    "Will you not then be mindful?": "He calls them to reflect and think carefully. If they understood the consequences, they would not make such unjust demands."
  },
  31: {
    "I do not say to you that I possess Allah's treasuries": "Noah clarifies his role with humility. He does not claim to control Allah's treasures or have the power to give wealth to whomever he wishes. He is simply a messenger, not a distributor of worldly provisions.",
    "or know the unseen,": "He does not claim knowledge of the unseen. Only Allah knows the future and hidden matters. Prophets only know what Allah reveals to them.",
    "nor do I claim to be an angel,": "He acknowledges he is human, not an angel. This was in response to their objection about him being \"just a human.\" Being human does not disqualify him from prophethood—rather, it makes him a relatable example for humanity.",
    "nor do I say that Allah will never grant goodness to those you look down upon.": "Noah refuses to judge the poor believers the way the arrogant chiefs do. He will not say that Allah will never bless them. Their outward poverty does not reflect their inner worth or their standing with Allah.",
    "Allah knows best what is ˹hidden˺ within them.": "Only Allah truly knows what is in people's hearts. The outward appearance of poverty or low status does not indicate a person's true value. The poor believers may have hearts full of faith that are more precious to Allah than all worldly wealth.",
    "˹If I did,˺ then I would truly be one of the wrongdoers.\"": "If Noah were to dismiss the believers or judge them unfairly based on their social status, he would be committing a grave wrong. He refuses to do so."
  },
  32: {
    "They protested, \"O Noah! You have argued with us far too much,": "After Noah presented his clear arguments and evidence, the disbelievers complained that he had debated with them excessively. Instead of reflecting on his proofs, they grew tired and annoyed.",
    "so bring upon us what you threaten us with,": "In their arrogance, they challenged Noah to bring the punishment he warned them about. This was a way of mocking him and expressing their complete disbelief in his warnings.",
    "if what you say is true.\"": "They conditioned their challenge on Noah proving his truthfulness by bringing the punishment immediately. This showed their complete rejection and mockery of the message."
  },
  33: {
    "He responded, \"It is Allah Who can bring it upon you if He wills,": "Noah clarified that he has no control over when or how punishment comes. Only Allah decides the timing. The punishment will come when Allah wills, not according to their demands.",
    "and then you will have no escape!": "When Allah's punishment does arrive, there will be no way to escape it. No mountain, shelter, or power will protect them from what Allah decrees."
  },
  34: {
    "My advice will not benefit you—no matter how hard I try—": "Noah acknowledges that even his best efforts to advise and guide them will be useless if they are determined to reject. A messenger can only convey; he cannot force hearts to accept.",
    "if Allah wills ˹for˺ you to stray.": "If Allah has willed that they remain astray because of their own persistent rejection and arrogance, then no amount of advice will help them. Guidance is ultimately from Allah, and He guides those who sincerely seek truth.",
    "He is your Lord, and to Him you will ˹all˺ be returned.\"": "Noah reminds them of the fundamental reality: Allah is their Lord and Master, and they will all return to Him for judgment. This should motivate them to reconsider their rejection."
  },
  35: {
    "Or do they say, \"He has fabricated this ˹Quran˺!\"?": "This verse shifts from the story of Noah to address the disbelievers of Makkah who accused Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) of inventing the Quran. The same accusation was made against many prophets.",
    "Say, ˹O Prophet,˺ \"If I have done so, then I bear the burden of that sin!": "The Prophet is instructed to respond: if he truly fabricated it, then the sin and its consequences would be upon him alone. He would face the punishment for such a grave lie.",
    "But I am free from your sinful accusation.\"": "The Prophet declares his innocence from their false accusations. He did not fabricate anything, and he is not responsible for their sins of lying and rejecting the truth. Each bears their own burden."
  },
  36: {
    "And it was revealed to Noah, \"None of your people will believe except those who already have.": "After 950 years of preaching, Allah informed Noah that no more people from his nation would accept faith. Those who were going to believe had already believed. This marked the end of the period of invitation.",
    "So do not be distressed by what they have been doing.\"": "Allah consoles Noah, telling him not to grieve or feel sad because of their persistent rejection and mockery. He had done his duty, and the outcome was in Allah's hands."
  },
  37: {
    "And build the Ark under Our ˹watchful˺ Eyes and directions,": "Allah commanded Noah to construct the Ark under divine supervision and guidance. Allah was watching over the process and directing Noah on how to build it. This was a miraculous undertaking guided by Allah Himself.",
    "and do not plead with Me for those who have done wrong,": "Allah warned Noah not to intercede for the wrongdoers or ask for them to be spared. Their fate was sealed because of their persistent rejection.",
    "for they will surely be drowned.\"": "The punishment was decreed: they would be destroyed by drowning. There was no changing this divine decree."
  },
  38: {
    "So he began to build the Ark,": "Noah obediently began constructing the Ark as Allah commanded. This was an act of tremendous faith, especially since his people had likely never seen a flood or perhaps even a large body of water.",
    "and whenever some of the chiefs of his people passed by, they mocked him.": "The arrogant leaders would pass by and ridicule Noah for building a massive ship on dry land. They thought he had lost his mind. Their mockery was relentless.",
    "He said, \"If you laugh at us, we will ˹soon˺ laugh at you similarly.": "Noah responded with confidence: your mockery will be turned around. When the flood comes and you realize the truth, we will have the last laugh. This wasn't vindictive pleasure, but a statement of certain justice."
  },
  39: {
    "You will soon come to know who will be visited by a humiliating torment ˹in this life˺": "Noah warned them that they would soon discover who would face disgraceful punishment in this world—the devastating flood that would humiliate them completely.",
    "and overwhelmed by an everlasting punishment ˹in the next˺.\"": "Beyond the worldly punishment, they would face eternal punishment in the Hereafter. The drowning was only the beginning; the lasting torment awaited them after death."
  },
  40: {
    "And when Our command came": "The moment of divine decree arrived. After all the warnings and preparation, Allah's command for the flood to begin was issued.",
    "and the oven burst ˹with water˺,": "This refers to a sign that marked the beginning of the flood. Water burst forth from the earth, possibly from an oven or a specific spot in the ground. This was the signal for Noah to act.",
    "We said ˹to Noah˺, \"Take into the Ark a pair from every species": "Allah commanded Noah to load the Ark with a male and female of every animal species to preserve life on earth after the flood. This was a miraculous undertaking guided by Allah.",
    "along with your family—except those against whom the decree ˹to drown˺ has already been passed—": "Noah was to take his family aboard, except for those family members upon whom the punishment had been decreed—specifically his disbelieving wife and son who had rejected the message.",
    "and those who believe.\"": "All the believers were to board the Ark with Noah, regardless of their social status or background.",
    "But none believed with him except for a few.": "Despite 950 years of preaching, only a small number had believed. Some reports mention around 80 people. This shows how few were saved despite the long period of invitation."
  },
  41: {
    "And he said, \"Board it!": "Noah commanded the believers to embark on the Ark as the flood began.",
    "In the Name of Allah it will sail and cast anchor.": "The ship's movement—both sailing and stopping—would be by Allah's name and permission. Noah entrusted their journey entirely to Allah, acknowledging that only He controls their safety.",
    "Surely my Lord is All-Forgiving, Most Merciful.\"": "Even at this moment of destruction for the disbelievers, Noah remembered Allah's mercy and forgiveness. For those who believed, Allah's mercy was saving them from the flood."
  },
  42: {
    "And ˹so˺ the Ark sailed with them through waves like mountains.": "The flood was catastrophic—the waves were as tall as mountains. The Ark, carrying Noah and the believers, navigated through this terrifying storm by Allah's protection.",
    "Noah called out to his son, who stood apart,": "Noah saw his son standing separately from the believers, not having boarded the Ark. Despite his son's disbelief, the father's love made Noah call out to him one last time.",
    "\"O my dear son! Come aboard with us": "Using tender, affectionate language, Noah pleaded with his son to join them on the Ark and be saved from the impending destruction.",
    "and do not be with the disbelievers.\"": "Noah urged his son to separate himself from the disbelievers who were about to perish. Even at the last moment, he hoped his son would choose faith and salvation."
  },
  43: {
    "He replied, \"I will take refuge on a mountain, which will protect me from the water.\"": "The son arrogantly believed he could save himself through his own means. He thought a high mountain would protect him from the flood, not understanding that this was Allah's decree that nothing could escape.",
    "Noah cried, \"Today no one is protected from Allah's decree except those to whom He shows mercy!\"": "Noah tried to make his son understand that this was not an ordinary flood that could be escaped by climbing higher ground. Only those whom Allah chose to save through the Ark would be protected.",
    "And the waves came between them,": "Before they could continue their exchange, the massive waves separated father and son. The opportunity for salvation had passed.",
    "and his son was among the drowned.": "Noah's son perished with the disbelievers. Despite being the son of a prophet, his disbelief sealed his fate. Family connection to a prophet does not guarantee salvation—only one's own faith matters."
  },
  44: {
    "And it was said, \"O earth! Swallow up your water.": "Allah commanded the earth to absorb the floodwater. The same earth that had released water now took it back in. This demonstrates Allah's absolute control over all creation.",
    "And O sky! Withhold ˹your rain˺.\"": "Allah commanded the sky to stop releasing rain. The heavens and earth obey Allah's commands instantly and completely.",
    "The floodwater receded": "The water levels dropped as the earth absorbed it and the rain stopped. The flood that had covered everything began to disappear.",
    "and the decree was carried out.": "Allah's command was fulfilled. The wrongdoers were destroyed, and the believers were saved. Everything happened exactly as Allah had planned.",
    "The Ark rested on Mount Judi,": "The Ark came to rest on Mount Judi, a mountain believed to be in the region of modern-day Turkey. This was where the new beginning for humanity would start.",
    "and it was said, \"Away with the wrongdoing people!\"": "A declaration was made cursing the destroyed wrongdoers—may they be far removed from Allah's mercy. Their end was a lesson for all future generations."
  },
  45: {
    "Noah called out to his Lord, saying,": "After the flood, Noah turned to Allah with a question weighing heavily on his heart regarding his drowned son.",
    "\"My Lord! Certainly my son is ˹also˺ of my family,": "Noah reminded Allah that his son was part of his family. He was confused because Allah had promised to save his family, yet his son had drowned.",
    "Your promise is surely true,": "Noah affirmed his belief that Allah's promises are always true and never fail.",
    "and You are the most just of all judges!\"": "Noah acknowledged Allah as the most just judge, subtly asking for an explanation about his son's fate. He was seeking understanding, not challenging Allah's decree."
  },
  46: {
    "Allah replied, \"O Noah! He is certainly not of your family": "Allah clarified that true family is defined by faith, not just blood. Noah's son, because of his disbelief, was not truly part of Noah's family in the spiritual sense that mattered for salvation.",
    "—he was entirely of unrighteous conduct.": "His son's actions and beliefs were completely unrighteous. His disbelief separated him from Noah's believing family despite the biological connection.",
    "So do not ask Me about what you have no knowledge of!": "Allah corrected Noah for asking about something beyond his understanding. Noah did not fully grasp that faith, not lineage, determines who is truly family in Allah's sight.",
    "I warn you so you do not fall into ignorance.\"": "This was a gentle warning and teaching moment for Noah. Allah was protecting him from making a request that would be inappropriate."
  },
  47: {
    "Noah pleaded, \"My Lord, I seek refuge in You from asking You about what I have no knowledge of,": "Noah immediately sought Allah's protection from ever again asking about matters beyond his knowledge. He accepted the correction with complete humility.",
    "and unless You forgive me and have mercy on me,": "Noah asked for Allah's forgiveness and mercy, recognizing that even prophets need Allah's grace and pardon.",
    "I will be one of the losers.\"": "Without Allah's forgiveness and mercy, Noah acknowledged he would be among the losers. This shows the humility of prophets—they never consider themselves above needing Allah's mercy."
  },
  48: {
    "It was said, \"O Noah! Disembark with Our peace and blessings on you": "Allah granted Noah peace and blessings as he left the Ark. His repentance was accepted, and he was honored.",
    "and some of the descendants of those with you.": "The blessings would extend to some of the offspring of the believers who were with Noah—those who would remain faithful.",
    "As for the others, We will allow them ˹a brief˺ enjoyment,": "Some future descendants would stray from the truth. Allah would allow them temporary enjoyment in this world.",
    "then they will be touched with a painful punishment from Us.\"": "After their brief worldly enjoyment, those who disbelieve would face painful punishment. This was a warning that the cycle of faith and disbelief would continue in future generations."
  },
  49: {
    "This is one of the stories of the unseen, which we reveal to you ˹O Prophet˺.": "Allah addresses Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), explaining that this detailed account of Noah is from the knowledge of the unseen that Allah has revealed to him.",
    "Neither you nor your people knew it before this.": "Before this revelation, neither the Prophet nor his people had knowledge of these specific details about Noah. The Arabs did not have this information, proving the Quran's divine origin.",
    "So be patient!": "The Prophet is encouraged to be patient in facing the rejection of his people, just as Noah was patient for 950 years. The stories of previous prophets provide comfort and lessons.",
    "Surely the ultimate outcome belongs ˹only˺ to the righteous.": "The final victory and success will always be for those who are righteous and God-fearing. No matter how long it takes, the believers will ultimately triumph."
  },
  50: {
    "And to the people of 'Âd We sent their brother Hûd.": "Allah sent Prophet Hud to the people of 'Ad, an ancient Arab tribe known for their great physical strength and impressive buildings. Hud was called their \"brother\" because he was from among them—a member of their own tribe.",
    "He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah.": "Hud's message was identical to Noah's and every other prophet: worship Allah alone. This is the universal message of all prophets.",
    "You have no god other than Him.": "There is no deity worthy of worship except Allah. All other objects of worship are false and have no power.",
    "You do nothing but fabricate lies ˹against Allah˺.\"": "By worshipping idols and claiming they were gods or partners with Allah, the people of 'Ad were inventing lies against Allah. Their entire religious system was based on falsehood."
  },
  51: {
    "O my people! I do not ask you for any reward for this ˹message˺.": "Like Noah and all prophets, Hud clarified that he was not seeking any worldly compensation for his mission. He had no financial or personal motive.",
    "My reward is only from the One Who created me.": "His payment and reward would come from Allah alone—the One who brought him into existence. This proved his sincerity and removed any suspicion of self-interest.",
    "Will you not then understand?": "Hud appeals to their reason: if he's not asking for anything from you, what possible motive would he have to lie? They should use their intellect to see his sincerity."
  },
  52: {
    "And O my people! Seek your Lord's forgiveness": "Hud urged his people to ask Allah for forgiveness for their idol worship and sins. Seeking forgiveness is the first step toward change.",
    "and turn to Him in repentance.": "After seeking forgiveness, they should sincerely repent—meaning they should stop their wrongdoing and commit to following the truth.",
    "He will shower you with rain in abundance,": "As a worldly benefit of repentance, Allah would send them plentiful rain. The people of 'Ad were experiencing drought, and Hud promised that returning to Allah would bring relief and provision.",
    "and add strength to your strength.": "Allah would increase their already famous physical strength if they believed and repented. Faith brings blessings in both spiritual and physical realms.",
    "So do not turn away, persisting in wickedness.\"": "Hud warned them not to reject the message and continue in their sinful ways. Turning away would only bring destruction upon them."
  },
  53: {
    "They argued, \"O Hûd! You have not given us any clear proof,": "The people of 'Ad rejected Prophet Hud's message by claiming he had not provided sufficient evidence for his prophethood and the truth of monotheism. This was a false claim, as Hud had presented clear arguments, signs, and warnings. However, their hearts were sealed by arrogance and attachment to their way of life. This is a common tactic of disbelievers throughout history—demanding proof while ignoring the proof already given. They witnessed Hud's impeccable character, his consistent message, and his lack of any worldly motive, yet they pretended none of this constituted evidence. True evidence requires an open heart to recognize it.",
    "and we will never abandon our gods upon your word,": "They stubbornly declared they would not leave their idol worship simply because Hud told them to. Their attachment to their false gods was based on tradition, pride, and tribal identity rather than any rational justification. They prioritized ancestral customs over divine truth. This reveals how powerful cultural attachment can be—people will cling to falsehood simply because it is familiar. They essentially said: 'Your word means nothing to us; our traditions mean everything.' This prioritizing of human tradition over divine revelation is a recurring theme in the rejection of prophets.",
    "nor will we believe in you.": "They completely closed the door to faith, declaring outright that they would never believe in Hud as a prophet. This was not a position based on evaluation of evidence but a predetermined rejection. Their minds were made up before any discussion began. This absolute refusal shows the depth of their arrogance and the hardness of their hearts. They were not seekers of truth but defenders of falsehood."
  },
  54: {
    "All we can say is that some of our gods have possessed you with evil.\"": "Unable to refute Hud's logical arguments, the people of 'Ad resorted to personal attacks. They accused him of being possessed or cursed by their idols, suggesting he had gone mad because he opposed their gods. This accusation served multiple purposes: it dismissed his message without engaging with it, it warned others not to follow him lest they too be 'cursed,' and it attributed power to their idols that they did not possess. This tactic of calling prophets mad or possessed was used against many messengers, including Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him). When people cannot defeat the message, they attack the messenger.",
    "He said, \"I call Allah to witness, and you too bear witness,": "Hud responded to their accusation with a powerful declaration. He invoked Allah as his witness—the Most High who sees and knows all things—and also called upon his opponents to witness his statement. This showed his complete confidence and sincerity. A person making a false claim would not invite the Almighty and his enemies to witness against him. Hud was so certain of his position that he wanted everyone to hear and remember what he was about to declare.",
    "that I totally reject whatever you associate": "Hud proclaimed his complete innocence and dissociation from their polytheism. He rejected entirely all the false gods they worshipped alongside Allah. This was a direct challenge to their accusation—far from being cursed by their gods, he openly denounced them as false and powerless. His rejection was total and uncompromising, using the word 'barī'' which means complete freedom and dissociation from something."
  },
  55: {
    "with Him in worship.": "Hud completed his declaration, making clear that he rejected everything they associated with Allah in worship. He worshipped Allah alone, without any partners. This is the essence of tawheed (monotheism)—the core message of every prophet. By declaring this openly before a hostile audience, Hud demonstrated the courage that comes from true faith.",
    "So let all of you plot against me without delay!": "In a remarkable display of confidence in Allah, Hud challenged his entire nation to do their worst against him. He told them to combine their efforts, use all their power and their idols' supposed power, and attack him immediately without giving him any respite. This was not arrogance but absolute trust in Allah's protection. Hud knew that no one could harm him without Allah's permission. This challenge also exposed the impotence of their idols—if they truly had power, surely they could harm the one who openly mocked them."
  },
  56: {
    "I have put my trust in Allah—my Lord and your Lord.": "Hud declared the source of his confidence: complete reliance on Allah (tawakkul). He emphasized that Allah is not only his Lord but also the Lord of his opponents, whether they acknowledged it or not. This reminder that Allah is everyone's Lord, including the disbelievers', is significant—it means they too are under His authority and cannot escape His power. Tawakkul is the fruit of true faith; when you truly believe in Allah's power and wisdom, you fear nothing else.",
    "There is no living creature that is not completely under His control.": "This powerful statement affirms Allah's absolute sovereignty over all creation. The Arabic phrase 'ākhidhun bi-nāṣiyatihā' literally means 'holding by its forelock'—the forelock being the front of the head, symbolizing complete control and dominance. Every creature, from the mightiest to the smallest, is entirely in Allah's grasp. He controls their life, death, sustenance, and every movement. This includes the powerful people of 'Ad who thought they were invincible. No matter how strong they considered themselves, they were as helpless before Allah as any other creature.",
    "Surely my Lord's Way is perfect justice.": "Allah's path—His management of affairs, His decrees, and His judgments—is characterized by perfect straightness and justice. He never wrongs anyone. Those who follow His path are guided to success, and those who oppose it face the consequences they deserve. This statement reassures Hud and the believers that they are on the right path and that justice will ultimately prevail. It also warns the disbelievers that their wrongdoing will not go unpunished."
  },
  57: {
    "But if you turn away, I have already delivered to you what I have been sent with.": "Hud made clear that he had fulfilled his prophetic duty. Whether they accepted or rejected the message, he had conveyed what Allah sent him to convey. A prophet's responsibility is to deliver the message clearly; guidance is ultimately in Allah's hands. This statement freed Hud from any blame for their disbelief and served as a final warning that they had no excuse before Allah. They could not claim ignorance on the Day of Judgment.",
    "My Lord will replace you with others.": "This is a profound warning: if they persist in disbelief, Allah will destroy them and bring another people in their place. Allah does not need any particular nation or people. If one group rejects His guidance, He will remove them and establish others who will be more deserving. This happened to 'Ad—they were utterly destroyed and replaced by other civilizations. This warning applies to all nations throughout history: no people are indispensable to Allah's plan.",
    "You are not harming Him in the least.": "Their disbelief and disobedience do not harm Allah in any way. He is completely self-sufficient and independent of His creation. If every human on earth disbelieved, it would not diminish Allah's dominion or glory in the slightest. Similarly, if everyone believed, it would not add anything to Him. They were only harming themselves by rejecting guidance. This truth removes any notion that humans can somehow affect Allah negatively through their disobedience.",
    "Indeed, my Lord is a vigilant Keeper over all things.": "Allah is Al-Hafīẓ—the Preserver, the Protector, the One who watches over and maintains everything. Nothing escapes His knowledge or care. He preserves the records of all deeds, protects the believers, and maintains the entire universe. This name implies that their crimes are being recorded and that Hud and the believers are under Allah's protection. The disbelievers cannot escape accountability because Allah is watching everything."
  },
  58: {
    "When Our command came,": "This phrase signals the arrival of divine punishment. When Allah's decree for destruction was issued, it was executed immediately and irresistibly. 'Our command' refers to Allah's decision that the time for 'Ad's punishment had come. After all warnings were rejected and all opportunities exhausted, the divine judgment was finalized.",
    "We rescued Hûd and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us,": "Before the punishment struck, Allah saved Hud and the believers from among his people. This salvation was purely by Allah's mercy—not because they could save themselves. The believers were few compared to the mighty nation of 'Ad, but they were the ones who mattered. Allah always protects those who believe in Him and follow His messengers, while the powerful disbelievers are destroyed despite their numbers and strength.",
    "saving them from a harsh torment.": "The torment that struck 'Ad was severe and harsh—a violent wind that lasted for days and destroyed everything in its path. The believers were saved from this devastating punishment. The word 'ghalīẓ' (harsh, severe) emphasizes the intensity of the punishment. The same word used to describe the torment indicates what would have been the believers' fate had they not followed Hud."
  },
  59: {
    "That was 'Âd.": "Allah summarizes the story of 'Ad with this declarative statement. This mighty civilization, known for their physical strength, impressive buildings, and worldly power, was now a lesson in history. Despite their greatness in worldly terms, they were destroyed because of their disbelief. The word 'tilka' (that) is used to point to something distant, indicating that 'Ad is now in the past, a destroyed people whose story serves as a warning.",
    "They denied the signs of their Lord,": "The first of their crimes listed is denying Allah's signs. These signs included the miracles shown through Hud, the natural signs in creation pointing to the Creator, and the revealed guidance calling them to truth. Denying these signs meant rejecting clear evidence and choosing blindness over sight.",
    "disobeyed His messengers,": "Although Hud was the messenger sent specifically to 'Ad, disobeying one messenger is equivalent to disobeying all messengers because they all came with the same fundamental message: worship Allah alone. Their rejection of Hud was a rejection of the entire institution of prophethood and divine guidance. This also indicates that they may have known of previous messengers and rejected their teachings as well.",
    "and followed the command of every stubborn tyrant.": "Instead of following the messenger of Allah, they followed their arrogant leaders who stubbornly opposed the truth. These tyrants led them astray, and the people willingly followed. The word 'jabbār' refers to someone who uses force and coercion, while ''anīd' means stubbornly resistant to truth. This is a warning about blind obedience to powerful leaders who oppose divine guidance. Following such leaders leads to destruction in this world and the next."
  },
  60: {
    "They were followed by a curse in this world,": "Allah's curse pursued 'Ad throughout their existence in this world. After their destruction, they became known as a cursed, destroyed nation—a byword for divine punishment. Their mention brings to mind Allah's wrath rather than any positive achievement. This curse means they were removed from Allah's mercy and became objects of disgrace and warning.",
    "as they will be on the Day of Judgment.": "The curse will continue into the Hereafter. On the Day of Judgment, they will be cursed again, publicly disgraced before all of creation, and then thrown into Hellfire. Their punishment is not only in this world but extends eternally. They face double disgrace: humiliation in this life as a destroyed nation and humiliation on the Day when all secrets are exposed.",
    "Surely 'Âd denied their Lord.": "Allah reiterates their fundamental crime: kufr (disbelief/denial) of their Lord. They knew Allah was their Creator but denied His right to be worshipped alone and rejected His messenger. This denial was the root of all their other sins. The word 'alā' (surely, indeed) emphasizes the certainty and gravity of their crime.",
    "So away with 'Âd, the people of Hûd.": "This is a statement of rejection and banishment. 'Bu'dan' means distance, removal, destruction—as if saying 'may they be far removed from mercy.' Allah declares them expelled from His mercy, destroyed and gone. They are identified as 'the people of Hud' to connect them to the prophet they rejected. This phrase serves as a final verdict on this nation."
  },
  61: {
    "And to the people of Thamûd We sent their brother Ṣâliḥ.": "Allah sent Prophet Salih to the people of Thamud, another ancient Arab nation. Like other prophets, Salih was from among his own people—their 'brother' in terms of lineage and familiarity. This made the message more accessible, as they knew his character and background. The Thamud lived in the area of Al-Hijr (modern-day Mada'in Salih in Saudi Arabia), where they carved homes in mountains. They succeeded 'Ad in time and were also known for their strength and civilization.",
    "He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah.": "Salih began with the same message as all prophets: worship Allah alone. This call to tawheed is the foundation of the divine message throughout history. He addressed them affectionately as 'my people,' showing his love and concern for them despite calling them away from their ancestral practices.",
    "You have no god other than Him.": "He clarified that Allah alone deserves worship—there is no other true god. All the idols they worshipped were false and powerless. This statement negates all false deities and affirms the oneness of Allah. It is a call to remove shirk (associating partners with Allah) from their lives completely.",
    "He is the One Who produced you from the earth and settled you on it.": "Salih presented evidence for why Allah alone deserves worship: He is the Creator who originated humanity from the earth (referring to Adam's creation from clay) and gave them the earth as a home to inhabit and develop. The word 'ansha'akum' (produced you) refers to bringing into existence, while 'ista'marakum' (settled you) means He made you inhabitants and developers of the earth. The One who created you and gave you your home has the sole right to your worship.",
    "So seek His forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance.": "After establishing Allah's right to worship, Salih called them to seek forgiveness for their past sins and to repent—to turn back to Allah with sincere hearts. Repentance (tawbah) means abandoning sin, regretting it, and resolving not to return to it. This combination of istighfār (seeking forgiveness) and tawbah (repentance) shows the path back to Allah's pleasure for those who have strayed.",
    "Surely my Lord is Ever Near, All-Responsive to prayers.\"": "Salih concluded with an encouraging description of Allah: He is Qarīb (Near) and Mujīb (Responsive). Despite His majesty and transcendence, Allah is near to His servants and responds to their prayers and repentance. This gives hope to sinners that if they turn back, Allah will accept them. He is not distant or uncaring—He hears every prayer and responds with wisdom and mercy."
  },
  62: {
    "They argued, \"O Ṣâliḥ! We truly had high hopes in you before this.": "The people of Thamud responded by expressing disappointment in Salih. Before he began calling them to monotheism, they had respected him and expected great things from him—perhaps leadership, wealth, or prestige among them. His call to abandon idol worship shattered their expectations. This shows that Salih was known for his good character and intelligence, which is why they had hoped he would be a conventional leader among them. When he became a prophet calling them away from their traditions, they felt betrayed.",
    "How dare you forbid us to worship what our forefathers had worshipped?": "They objected to Salih prohibiting their ancestral idol worship. To them, the practices of their forefathers were sacred and unquestionable. This blind following of tradition without examining its validity is a recurring problem throughout human history. They saw Salih's call as an attack on their heritage and identity. The question 'how dare you' shows their indignation—they could not believe one of their own would challenge their time-honored practices.",
    "We are certainly in alarming doubt about what you are inviting us to.\"": "They claimed to have deep, troubling doubts about the monotheism Salih preached. The word 'murīb' indicates doubt that causes anxiety and suspicion. However, this was likely an excuse rather than genuine intellectual uncertainty. If they had truly wanted answers, they would have asked questions and sought understanding. Instead, they used 'doubt' as a shield against accepting the truth. Genuine seekers find their way to truth; those who use doubt as an excuse remain in darkness by choice."
  },
  63: {
    "He responded, \"O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord": "Salih asked them to reflect deeply on his position. What if he truly was standing on clear evidence from Allah—divine revelation, certain knowledge, and truth? This rhetorical question invites them to consider the possibility that he might be right. It is a call to intellectual honesty: set aside your prejudices and consider the evidence. A person with clear proof from Allah is in a completely different position than someone promoting personal opinion.",
    "and He has blessed me with a mercy from Him.": "This 'mercy' refers to prophethood itself—the greatest blessing Allah can bestow on a human being. Salih acknowledged that his role as a prophet was a divine mercy and favor, not something he achieved on his own. This mercy also includes the guidance, knowledge, and support that comes with prophethood. He presented himself humbly as a recipient of Allah's blessing, not as someone claiming superiority through personal achievement.",
    "Who could help me against Allah if I were to disobey Him?": "Salih posed a powerful question: if Allah has commanded him to deliver this message, who could protect him from Allah's punishment if he failed to do so or altered the message to please people? No one can protect anyone from Allah. Even if the entire nation opposed him, their opposition was nothing compared to the consequence of disobeying Allah. This shows a prophet's primary concern is Allah's pleasure, not people's approval.",
    "You would only contribute to my doom.": "If Salih were to follow their wishes and abandon his prophetic mission, their support would not benefit him—it would only add to his loss and destruction. By pressuring him to compromise his message, they were essentially trying to lead him to ruin. True loss is not losing worldly status or facing persecution; true loss is losing Allah's pleasure and facing His punishment."
  },
  64: {
    "And O my people! This she-camel of Allah is a sign for you.": "Allah provided Thamud with a miraculous sign: a she-camel that emerged from a rock by Allah's power. This was not an ordinary camel but a divine miracle specifically created as proof of Salih's prophethood. The camel belonged to Allah in a special sense—it was His sign and under His protection. The Thamud had demanded a miracle, and Allah gave them one. Now they had no excuse for disbelief.",
    "So leave her to graze freely on Allah's earth": "The only condition attached to this miracle was simple: let the camel graze freely on the land. She would eat from the vegetation that Allah had created. They were not required to feed her or care for her—just to leave her alone. The earth belongs to Allah, and He can designate portions of it for whomever He wills. This was an easy test, but their arrogance would not allow them to pass it.",
    "and do her no harm,": "Salih explicitly warned them not to harm the she-camel in any way. Touching her with evil intent or hurting her would be a direct transgression against Allah's sign. This prohibition was clear and unambiguous—they could not later claim they did not know. The warning was delivered before any violation, giving them no excuse.",
    "or a swift punishment will overtake you!": "The consequence of harming the she-camel was clearly stated: immediate divine punishment. The word 'qarīb' (near, swift) indicates that the punishment would come quickly, without delay. This was not a vague, distant threat but an imminent consequence. They knew exactly what would happen if they transgressed. Yet despite this clear warning, they chose to defy Allah."
  },
  65: {
    "But they killed her,": "Despite all warnings, the people of Thamud killed the miraculous she-camel. One man, Qudār ibn Sālif, struck her with a sword, but the act represented the will of the entire community who approved of and encouraged it. This was an act of supreme arrogance and defiance—killing a clear sign from Allah. They thought they could challenge divine authority and get away with it. Their action sealed their fate.",
    "so he warned them, \"You have only three more days to enjoy life in your homes—": "After they killed the she-camel, Salih delivered the final warning: they had exactly three days left to live. This grace period allowed them time to reflect, repent, or prepare—though their fate was now sealed. The precision of the timing—three days—was itself a sign. If the punishment came exactly as predicted, it would prove beyond doubt that Salih spoke the truth. They would spend these three days watching for signs of the coming doom.",
    "this is an unfailing promise!\"": "Salih emphasized that this was not an empty threat but a promise that would definitely be fulfilled. The word 'makdhūb' means something proven false—Salih declared this promise would not be proven false. Allah's promises always come true. The people could either spend their remaining days in denial or use them to repent and face their fate with acceptance. Most chose denial."
  },
  66: {
    "When Our command came,": "After the three-day respite, Allah's command for punishment was executed. The phrase indicates the transition from warning to action—the time for chances had ended, and the time for consequences had arrived. 'Our command' refers to Allah's decree that their destruction should occur.",
    "We saved Ṣâliḥ and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us": "Before the punishment struck, Allah saved Salih and the small group of believers. Just as with Hud and his followers, this salvation was attributed to Allah's mercy, not to any power of their own. The believers were outnumbered and powerless against the majority, but Allah's mercy was sufficient. This pattern repeats throughout prophetic history: the believers are saved while the disbelieving majority is destroyed.",
    "and spared them the disgrace of that Day.": "The believers were saved not only from physical destruction but also from the humiliation and disgrace that befell the disbelievers. Being destroyed by divine punishment is the ultimate disgrace—a public declaration of Allah's wrath. The believers were spared this shame and instead received honor through their faith and salvation.",
    "Surely your Lord alone is the All-Powerful, Almighty.": "Allah concludes with two of His names: Al-Qawiyy (The All-Powerful, possessing perfect strength) and Al-'Azīz (The Almighty, The Invincible). These names are particularly relevant here—Allah had the power to destroy the mighty Thamud and the invincibility to execute His will despite any opposition. No matter how strong Thamud thought they were, Allah's strength is infinitely greater."
  },
  67: {
    "And the mighty blast overtook the wrongdoers,": "The punishment came in the form of a 'ṣayhah'—a mighty blast or scream. This could have been an earthquake accompanied by a terrifying sound, or a thunderous noise that killed them. The description emphasizes its overwhelming power. The wrongdoers, who had arrogantly defied Allah, were suddenly helpless before this divine punishment. The term 'wrongdoers' (ẓālamīn) indicates they brought this upon themselves through their injustice.",
    "so they fell lifeless in their homes,": "The blast struck them dead where they were, in their very homes. The Arabic 'jāthimīn' describes bodies fallen forward on their knees and faces—a posture of complete collapse. The homes they were so proud of, carved into mountains for security, became their tombs. All their strength, wealth, and architecture could not protect them from Allah's punishment. They died instantly, their arrogance silenced forever."
  },
  68: {
    "as if they had never lived there.": "After the punishment, it was as if Thamud had never existed. Their civilization was erased so completely that no trace of their vibrant life remained. This is a sobering image: a nation that was powerful and established, gone as if they never were. All their achievements, all their building, all their pride—reduced to nothing. This is the fate of those who oppose Allah. Their worldly accomplishments amount to nothing when they stand against divine truth.",
    "Surely Thamûd denied their Lord,": "Just as with 'Ad, their core crime is restated: denial of Allah. Their rejection of His signs, His messenger, and His guidance was the root cause of their destruction. All their other sins stemmed from this fundamental rejection. The repetition of 'alā' (surely, indeed) emphasizes the certainty of this statement.",
    "so away with Thamûd!": "The same phrase of rejection and banishment used for 'Ad is repeated for Thamud: 'bu'dan li-Thamūd'—away with Thamud, may they be removed from mercy. This is the final verdict on this nation. They are gone, destroyed, and cursed—a permanent lesson for all who come after them. The ruins of their dwellings in Mada'in Salih still stand today as a testament to this divine judgment."
  },
  69: {
    "And surely Our messenger-angels came to Abraham with good news of a son.": "The narrative shifts to Prophet Ibrahim (Abraham), peace be upon him. Angels came to him in the form of men, sent by Allah with the good news of a son (Ishaq/Isaac) who would be born to him and his elderly wife Sarah. This visit had multiple purposes: delivering good news, testing Ibrahim's hospitality, and announcing the impending destruction of the people of Lot. The mention of 'Our messenger-angels' (rusulunā) indicates they were sent on an official divine mission.",
    "They greeted him with, \"Peace!\"": "The angels greeted Ibrahim with 'salām'—the greeting of peace. This is the greeting of the believers, established since the earliest times. The angels' use of this greeting showed they came in peace and with good intentions. It was also a sign of honor and respect for this great prophet.",
    "And he replied, \"Peace be upon you!\"": "Ibrahim returned the greeting, also saying 'salām.' According to scholars, his reply was even better than their greeting—he said 'salāmun' (peace) which is grammatically more emphatic and lasting than their 'salāman.' This shows Ibrahim's excellence in manners and his immediate welcoming of guests, even strangers. The exchange of peace greetings establishes a foundation of goodwill.",
    "Then it was not long before he brought them a fat, roasted calf.": "Ibrahim quickly went to prepare a meal for his guests. He chose a calf—a valuable animal—and had it roasted. The word 'ḥanīdh' indicates it was roasted with hot stones or in an earth oven, making it tender and delicious. The phrase 'mā labitha' (it was not long) shows his eagerness and efficiency in serving guests. This demonstrates Ibrahim's legendary hospitality—he gave his best to unexpected guests without hesitation. He did not ask who they were or why they came before serving them. This teaches us the Islamic etiquette of honoring guests."
  },
  70: {
    "And when he saw that their hands did not reach for the food,": "Ibrahim noticed something strange: the guests did not eat. In Arab culture, refusing to eat offered food could indicate hostile intentions—a guest who accepts food is bound by hospitality to mean no harm. Their refusal to eat made Ibrahim uneasy. He had offered his best food, and their abstention was unusual and concerning. This detail shows Ibrahim's attentiveness and wisdom in reading social cues.",
    "he became suspicious and fearful of them.": "Ibrahim felt 'nakirahum' (he found them strange, unfamiliar) and 'awjasa minhum khīfah' (he felt fear within himself). His concern was understandable—three strangers who refused food could be enemies. Despite being a great prophet, Ibrahim experienced normal human emotions. His fear was not a weakness but a natural response to an unusual situation. However, he maintained his composure and did not act impulsively.",
    "They reassured him, \"Do not be afraid!": "The angels quickly perceived Ibrahim's concern and reassured him. They told him not to fear, indicating they meant him no harm. This kind reassurance shows that angels, when appearing to prophets, communicate in ways that establish trust and remove anxiety. They were about to deliver important news, and Ibrahim needed to be at ease to receive it.",
    "We are angels sent only against the people of Lot.\"": "The angels revealed their identity and their mission. They were not ordinary travelers but angels sent by Allah. Their mission was to execute divine punishment on the people of Lot (Sodom and Gomorrah), who had persisted in grave sins, particularly homosexuality and highway robbery. Ibrahim had nothing to fear because the punishment was not for him but for the wicked cities nearby. This revelation set the stage for Ibrahim's subsequent intercession on behalf of any believers among Lot's people and the rescue of Lot and his family."
  },
71: {
  "And his wife was standing by, so she laughed,": "Sarah, the wife of Ibrahim, was present during the angels' visit, standing nearby, perhaps serving the guests or observing the unusual scene. When she heard the angels reassure Ibrahim or when she learned of their mission against the wicked people of Lot, she laughed. Some scholars say she laughed out of joy at the news of punishment coming to the corrupt people. Others say she laughed in amazement at the strange events unfolding—guests who would not eat, the revelation that they were angels, and the news they brought. Her laughter indicated engagement with the conversation and set the stage for the surprising announcement to follow.",
  "then We gave her good news of the birth of Isaac,": "Allah gave Sarah the wonderful news that she would bear a son named Isaac (Ishaq). This was miraculous because Sarah was elderly and had been barren her entire life. She had given up hope of having children and had even given her servant Hajar to Ibrahim so he could have offspring (resulting in Ismail). Now, in her old age, Allah granted her the child she had longed for. This demonstrates that Allah's power is unlimited—He gives children to whom He wills, regardless of natural circumstances.",
  "and, after him, Jacob.": "The good news extended even further: not only would she have Isaac, but Isaac would have a son named Jacob (Ya'qub), meaning she would live to see her grandson. This promise of Jacob indicated longevity for both Sarah and Isaac, and it assured them that their lineage would continue. Jacob would become the father of the twelve tribes of Israel. This comprehensive blessing showed Allah's abundant favor upon Ibrahim's family."
},
72: {
  "She wondered, \"Oh, my! How can I have a child in this old age,": "Sarah's reaction was one of utter amazement. The expression 'yā waylatā' is an exclamation of astonishment, roughly meaning 'Oh, my!' or 'Woe to me!' She was not complaining but expressing how incredible this news was. How could she, an elderly woman well past childbearing age, possibly conceive and give birth? From a purely natural perspective, this seemed impossible. Her reaction was human and understandable—even the wife of a great prophet found such news hard to fathom.",
  "and my husband here is an old man?": "Sarah pointed to Ibrahim, noting that he too was elderly. Even if she were somehow capable of bearing children, her husband was an old man. Both biological factors seemed to work against the possibility of conception. This honest acknowledgment of their physical condition makes the miracle even more evident—when the child came, it would be clearly through Allah's power, not natural means.",
  "This is truly an astonishing thing!\"": "She concluded by declaring this news to be truly strange and wondrous. The word ''ajīb' indicates something extraordinary, unprecedented, and amazing. Her statement was not one of denial or disbelief but of sheer wonder at Allah's decree. It showed she understood the miraculous nature of what was being promised."
},
73: {
  "They responded, \"Are you astonished by Allah's decree?": "The angels gently corrected Sarah's amazement. They asked rhetorically whether she was surprised by what Allah had decreed. The implication was that nothing should surprise someone when it comes to Allah's power. He who created the heavens and earth, who gives life and causes death, is certainly able to grant a child to elderly parents. This question was a reminder that human limitations do not constrain divine ability.",
  "May Allah's mercy and blessings be upon you, O people of this house.": "The angels invoked Allah's mercy (raḥmah) and blessings (barakāt) upon Ibrahim's household. This was a prophetic blessing recognizing the special status of this family. Ibrahim's house was a house of prophethood, worship, and righteousness. The angels' blessing affirmed their honored position with Allah and served as a reassurance that the promised child would indeed come. This household was chosen for divine favor.",
  "Indeed, He is Praiseworthy, All-Glorious.\"": "The angels concluded with two of Allah's beautiful names: Al-Ḥamīd (the Praiseworthy, worthy of all praise for His perfection and favors) and Al-Majīd (the All-Glorious, full of honor, nobility, and generosity). These names are particularly appropriate here—Allah is praiseworthy for the incredible favor He was bestowing, and His glory is manifest in His ability to do what seems impossible to humans. These attributes remind us that Allah deserves praise for every blessing and that His glorious nature includes unlimited power and generosity."
},
74: {
  "Then after the fear had left Abraham, and the good news had reached him,": "Once Ibrahim understood that his guests were angels, his initial fear subsided. The good news of Isaac's birth also filled his heart with joy. With these anxieties resolved, Ibrahim's natural compassion emerged. He was no longer worried about himself or his family—now his thoughts turned to others who might be affected by the angels' mission.",
  "he began to plead with Us for the people of Lot.": "Learning that the angels were sent to destroy the people of Lot, Ibrahim began to argue and plead on their behalf. He did not simply accept the destruction without advocating for mercy. This pleading (mujādalah) showed Ibrahim's tender heart—he hoped that perhaps the people might be spared or that the believers among them would be saved. His nephew Lot was among those people, and Ibrahim cared deeply about what would happen to them. This intercession demonstrated Ibrahim's role as a compassionate patriarch concerned with the welfare of others."
},
75: {
  "Truly, Abraham was forbearing, tender-hearted,": "Allah praised Ibrahim with two beautiful characteristics. 'Ḥalīm' means forbearing, patient, and slow to anger—Ibrahim did not react hastily or harshly even when faced with difficult situations. 'Awwāh' means one who sighs or moans in devotion, tender-hearted, deeply moved by spiritual matters, and constantly turning to Allah in supplication. Ibrahim felt deeply for others and expressed his emotions in prayer and worship. These qualities made him plead for even a sinful people facing destruction.",
  "and ever turning to his Lord.": "'Munīb' means one who constantly returns to Allah, always coming back in repentance, devotion, and reliance. Ibrahim was not self-reliant but always turned to Allah in every matter. His pleading for Lot's people was itself a form of turning to Allah—he brought his concerns before his Lord rather than accepting circumstances passively. This combination of forbearance, tender-heartedness, and constant turning to Allah made Ibrahim the exemplary friend of Allah (Khalīlullāh)."
},
76: {
  "The angels said, \"O Abraham! Plead no more!": "The angels stopped Ibrahim's intercession. While his compassion was noble, the decision had already been made. There comes a point when pleading is no longer appropriate—when Allah's decree has been finalized. Ibrahim's advocacy was noted and appreciated, but it could not change what had been decided. This teaches us that while supplication and intercession are encouraged, we must also accept Allah's final decisions.",
  "Your Lord's decree has already come,": "The command for punishment had already been issued. This was not a future possibility but a present reality—the decree had come. The people of Lot had exhausted their opportunities, and the time for mercy had passed. When Allah's command comes, nothing can delay or prevent it. This finality should serve as a warning to all who think they have unlimited time to repent.",
  "and they will certainly be afflicted with a punishment that cannot be averted!\"": "The punishment was guaranteed and unavoidable. No intercession, no pleading, no escape plan could prevent what was coming. The word 'ghayr mardūd' means it cannot be turned back or repelled. Once Allah decides to punish a people, there is no force in the universe that can stop it. This was the fate of those who persisted in their abominations despite clear warnings from Prophet Lot."
},
77: {
  "When Our messenger-angels came to Lot,": "The same angels who visited Ibrahim continued their journey to the cities of Lot's people. They appeared as handsome young men, which would soon cause great distress given the nature of the people they were visiting. This was also part of the divine plan—their appearance would expose the wickedness of the people one final time before their destruction.",
  "he was distressed and worried by their arrival.": "Unlike Ibrahim who was merely surprised, Lot was deeply troubled by the arrival of these guests. He knew his people's evil practices—they would assault any attractive strangers who entered the city. The Arabic 'sī'a bihim' indicates he was grieved on their account, and 'ḍāqa bihim dhar'an' means he felt constrained, anxious, and unable to protect them. He faced an impossible situation: his duty as a host required him to protect guests, but his people's wickedness made that nearly impossible.",
  "He said, \"This is a terrible day.\"": "Lot immediately recognized the danger. He called it 'yawm 'aṣīb'—a day of difficulty, hardship, and calamity. He foresaw the confrontation that would come when his people learned of these beautiful guests. His distress shows the burden prophets carry when they must live among corrupt people while maintaining their own righteousness and trying to protect others."
},
78: {
  "And the men of his people—who were used to shameful deeds—came to him rushing.": "News of the handsome visitors spread quickly, and the men of the city rushed to Lot's home with evil intentions. The phrase 'wa min qablu kānū ya'malūna al-sayyi'āt' reminds us that this was their established practice—they were habituated to this shameful sin of homosexuality. They came rushing (yuhra'ūn), driven by their base desires, showing no shame or restraint. This scene exposes the depth of their moral corruption.",
  "He pleaded, \"O my people! Here are my daughters for marriage—they are pure for you.": "In desperation, Lot offered a lawful alternative—marriage to the women of his community, whom he called 'my daughters' either referring to his actual daughters or, as some scholars say, the women of his nation whom a prophet may call his daughters. He described them as 'aṭhar' (purer) for them—meaning marriage to women was the pure, natural, and lawful way ordained by Allah. This offer emphasized that there was a legitimate way to fulfill desires, but they had to abandon their perversion.",
  "So fear Allah, and do not humiliate me by disrespecting my guests.": "Lot appealed to their fear of Allah—if they had any—and to their sense of honor. Violating a man's guests was a tremendous dishonor to the host in Arab culture. By harming his guests, they would be humiliating Lot himself. He hoped that even if they did not fear Allah, perhaps social shame might restrain them.",
  "Is there not even a single right-minded man among you?\"": "This rhetorical question expressed Lot's despair. Was there not even one man with reason, righteousness, and good sense among them? Not one who would stand against this evil or support him in protecting his guests? The question highlighted the complete moral collapse of their society—not a single righteous voice remained among the men of the city."
},
79: {
  "They argued, \"You certainly know that we have no need for your daughters.": "The men brazenly rejected Lot's offer. They stated plainly that they had no interest in women—a shocking admission of their unnatural desires. They felt no shame in declaring this perversion publicly. This open rejection of the natural way showed how deeply their corruption had taken hold. Their hearts had been so twisted that they found the lawful unappealing.",
  "You already know what we desire!\"": "They accused Lot of already knowing what they wanted—the male guests he was protecting. Their brazenness was complete; they did not even try to hide their intentions. This statement condemned them from their own mouths. They knew Lot was a prophet who preached against their behavior, yet they openly declared their intention to commit sin. This was rebellion against Allah with full knowledge."
},
80: {
  "He responded, \"If only I had the strength to resist you": "Lot expressed his helplessness. He wished he had enough physical power to repel them himself. As a prophet, he was likely not a warrior, and he was vastly outnumbered. This human moment shows the genuine distress of a righteous man surrounded by wickedness. He was not being weak in faith but honestly acknowledging his physical limitation in the face of a mob.",
  "or could rely on a strong supporter.\"": "He wished he had a powerful tribe, clan, or supporter to back him up. In Arabian society, a man's strength often came from his family connections. Lot was a stranger among these people—he had come with Ibrahim and settled there as an outsider. He had no tribal support to call upon. This statement became a point of prophetic teaching, as the Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) later said: 'May Allah have mercy on Lot; he was seeking refuge in a strong supporter'—meaning Allah Himself, the strongest supporter of all. Lot's despair was human, but he would soon learn that Allah's support was sufficient."
},
81: {
  "The angels said, \"O Lot! We are the messengers of your Lord.": "At this critical moment, the angels revealed their identity. They were not vulnerable human guests but powerful messengers of Allah sent on a divine mission. This revelation transformed the situation entirely. Lot was not alone, and his guests did not need his protection—they were capable of protecting him and destroying his enemies.",
  "They will never reach you.": "The angels assured Lot that the mob would never be able to harm him or reach his guests. Despite the threatening crowd outside, Lot and his family were completely safe under divine protection. The angels then struck the mob with blindness, as mentioned in other verses (15:72, 54:37), so they could not find the door. The would-be assaulters stumbled about in confusion, their evil plans frustrated.",
  "So travel with your family in the dark of night,": "Lot was instructed to leave the city with his family under cover of darkness. The punishment would come at dawn, and they needed to be far away. Traveling at night would also help them leave unnoticed. This was the practical instruction for salvation—those who believed needed to physically remove themselves from the place of destruction.",
  "and do not let any of you look back, except your wife.": "A strange command accompanied the instruction: no one should look back at the city as they fled. This was perhaps a test of obedience and trust—to look back might indicate longing for what was being left behind or doubt about the command. However, Lot's wife was excepted from the saved group.",
  "She will certainly suffer the fate of the others.": "Lot's wife, despite being married to a prophet, was not a believer. She sympathized with the wicked people and their ways. When the punishment came, she would be among those destroyed. Some narrations say she looked back and was struck by the punishment. Her fate is a warning that family connections to righteous people do not guarantee salvation—each person must believe and obey for themselves.",
  "Their appointed time is the morning. Is the morning not near?\"": "The angels specified the time of destruction: morning (ṣubḥ). They asked rhetorically, 'Is the morning not near?'—indicating that the punishment was imminent, only hours away. This question also served to reassure Lot: he did not need to endure much longer. The night of terror would soon end, and justice would come with the dawn."
},
82: {
  "When Our command came,": "The appointed morning arrived, and Allah's command was executed. This phrase, repeated throughout Surah Hud, signals the transition from warning to punishment. The time for repentance had expired, and divine judgment was carried out.",
  "We turned the cities upside down": "The punishment was cataclysmic—Allah literally inverted their cities, lifting them up and then smashing them down. 'Jā'alnā 'āliyahā sāfilahā' means their upper parts became their lower parts. This could describe a massive earthquake, a volcanic eruption, or a miraculous overturning. The comfortable cities they had built for their sinful pleasures became inverted ruins. The physical inversion symbolized their moral inversion—they had turned natural law upside down, and now their world was turned upside down upon them.",
  "and rained down on them clustered stones of baked clay,": "In addition to the overturning, Allah rained stones upon them. These were not ordinary rocks but 'ḥijārah min sijjīl'—stones of baked clay, hard and devastating. The word 'manḍūd' indicates they came in layers, one after another, a relentless bombardment. These stones had been prepared specifically for this punishment. The people who had rained sins upon their society were now rained upon with destruction."
},
83: {
  "marked by your Lord O Prophet.": "Each stone was 'musawwamah'—marked or designated by Allah. Some scholars say each stone had the name of its specific victim written on it. Others say they were marked to distinguish them as instruments of divine punishment rather than natural stones. Either way, this emphasizes that the destruction was targeted and purposeful, not random. Allah's punishment is precise and just.",
  "And these stones are not far from the pagan wrongdoers!": "This warning extends beyond Lot's people to the polytheists of Mecca and all wrongdoers throughout time. The same punishment—or something like it—is not far from those who commit similar crimes or persist in wrongdoing. The ruins of Lot's cities (believed to be near the Dead Sea) were along trade routes known to the Quraysh. Every time they passed by, they could see evidence of Allah's punishment. This verse warns that divine retribution is never far from those who deserve it."
},
84: {
  "And to the people of Midian We sent their brother Shu'aib.": "The narrative continues with Prophet Shu'aib, sent to the people of Midian (Madyan). Like previous prophets, he was from among his own people—their 'brother' in lineage. Midian was a commercial center, and its people were known as successful traders. Shu'aib is sometimes identified as the father-in-law of Musa (Moses). His people's primary sins were religious (idolatry) and economic (fraud).",
  "He said, \"O my people! Worship Allah. You have no god other than Him.": "Shu'aib began with the universal message of all prophets: worship Allah alone. This call to tawheed (monotheism) is the foundation of divine guidance. Like Hud and Salih before him, he called his people away from their idols to the one true God. Without correcting belief, no other reform is meaningful.",
  "And do not give short measure and weight.": "Beyond the theological message, Shu'aib addressed their specific sin: commercial fraud. They would cheat in measurements, giving less than what was due when selling and taking more than entitled when buying. This is a form of theft that corrupts entire economies and societies. Shu'aib's combination of religious and ethical reform shows that Islam addresses both faith and action.",
  "I do see you in prosperity now,": "He acknowledged their current wealth and comfort. Business was good, and they were prospering—at least in worldly terms. This prosperity, however, was built on fraud and injustice. Shu'aib wanted them to understand that their wealth would not protect them from Allah's punishment. In fact, their prosperity made their ingratitude and disobedience even worse.",
  "but I truly fear for you the torment of an overwhelming Day.": "Despite their current comfort, Shu'aib warned them of a coming day of punishment—whether in this world or the next—that would encompass them entirely. The word 'muḥīṭ' describes a punishment that surrounds them completely with no escape. Their fraudulent gains would not benefit them against Allah's judgment."
},
85: {
  "O my people! Give full measure and weigh with justice.": "Shu'aib emphasized the positive command: be honest in all transactions. Give people exactly what they are owed. Use accurate scales and measures. Business ethics are an integral part of religious practice in Islam. The Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) said: 'The honest merchant will be with the prophets, the truthful, and the martyrs' (Tirmidhi). This verse establishes justice in commerce as a divine command.",
  "Do not defraud people of their property,": "Beyond just measuring correctly, they must not cheat people in any way—through deception, manipulation, or any means of taking what belongs to others unjustly. The word 'tabkhasū' means to diminish or reduce someone's rightful due. This prohibition covers all forms of economic injustice, not just measurement fraud.",
  "nor go about spreading corruption in the land.": "Their individual acts of fraud contributed to broader social corruption. When dishonesty becomes normalized, it spreads through society like a disease. Shu'aib warned them not to be agents of this corruption. Economic crimes do not exist in isolation—they destroy trust, harm the vulnerable, and corrupt the moral fabric of society."
},
86: {
  "What is left as a lawful gain by Allah is far better for you if you are truly believers.": "'Baqiyyatullāh' refers to the lawful profit that remains after honest dealing. Even though it may seem like less than what could be gained through fraud, it is better in every way. It is blessed by Allah, free from guilt, and will not bring punishment. This lawful remainder contains barakah (divine blessing) that makes it more beneficial than ill-gotten abundance. True believers recognize this and prefer modest lawful earnings over greater unlawful gains.",
  "And I am not a keeper over you.\"": "Shu'aib clarified his role: he was a warner and guide, not a controller or enforcer. He could not force them to be honest or prevent them from cheating. His job was to deliver the message and advise them. The choice—and its consequences—was theirs. This statement also relieved him of responsibility for their actions; he had fulfilled his duty by warning them."
},
87: {
  "They asked sarcastically, \"O Shu'aib! Does your prayer command you": "The people of Midian responded with mockery. They sarcastically asked if his prayer (ṣalāh) was telling him to preach these things. By mentioning his prayer, they might have been ridiculing his religious devotion or implying that his 'worship' was making him give them unwanted advice. Their sarcasm revealed their contempt for both his message and his piety.",
  "that we should abandon what our forefathers worshipped": "They objected to abandoning their ancestral idols. This attachment to tradition regardless of truth is a recurring theme in the Quran. They preferred inherited falsehood over revealed truth. Their forefathers' practices were sacred to them, even though those practices involved worshipping powerless idols.",
  "or give up managing our wealth as we please?": "They also objected to being told how to conduct their business. They saw their wealth as entirely their own, to be handled however they wished. The idea that Allah had rights over their economic behavior offended their sense of autonomy. This attitude persists today—people often accept religious obligations in 'spiritual' matters but resist divine guidance in financial affairs.",
  "Indeed, you are such a tolerant, sensible man!\"": "This was pure sarcasm. They mockingly called him 'ḥalīm' (forbearing, patient) and 'rashīd' (rightly guided, sensible), meaning the opposite. They were saying: 'You think you're so wise and patient, telling us what to do.' Their mockery showed they had no respect for him or his message despite his genuine wisdom and patience."
},
88: {
  "He said, \"O my people! Consider if I stand on a clear proof from my Lord": "Shu'aib responded to their mockery with the same argument used by previous prophets. He asked them to consider the possibility that he was standing on genuine evidence from Allah. What if his message was truly from God? Would they still dismiss it so casually? This rhetorical question invited reflection rather than knee-jerk rejection.",
  "and He has blessed me with a good provision from Him.": "Unlike prophets they might accuse of preaching for personal gain, Shu'aib acknowledged that Allah had already provided for him well. He was not preaching economic ethics because he was poor and jealous of their wealth. His 'rizq ḥasan' (good provision) meant he had no material motive for his message. He practiced what he preached and was blessed for it.",
  "I do not want to do what I am forbidding you from.": "Shu'aib emphasized his consistency: he did not engage in the fraudulent practices he forbade. He was not a hypocrite who preached one thing and did another. His personal example matched his message. This is essential for any reformer—credibility comes from practicing what you preach.",
  "I only intend reform to the best of my ability.": "His sole intention was 'iṣlāḥ'—reform, improvement, putting things right. He was not seeking power, wealth, or status. He simply wanted to correct the wrongs in his society. The phrase 'mā istaṭa'tu' (to the best of my ability) shows humility—he could only do what was within his capacity. The results were up to Allah.",
  "My success comes only through Allah.": "Shu'aib acknowledged that any success he might achieve was entirely from Allah. 'Tawfīq' (success, guidance to what is right) comes only from Allah. This statement negated any claim to self-sufficiency or personal power. He was merely an instrument; Allah was the source of all good.",
  "In Him I trust and to Him I turn.": "He concluded by affirming his complete reliance on Allah (tawakkul) and his constant returning to Allah (inābah). These twin attitudes of trust and repentance characterized his relationship with his Lord. Whatever the people decided, Shu'aib's orientation was toward Allah."
},
89: {
  "O my people! Do not let your opposition to me lead you to a fate": "Shu'aib warned them against allowing their hatred of him to bring about their destruction. The word 'shiqāqī' refers to opposition, hostility, and enmity toward him. Sometimes people reject truth simply because they dislike the messenger. Shu'aib urged them not to let personal animosity blind them to the consequences of their actions.",
  "similar to that of the people of Noah, or Hûd, or Ṣâliḥ.": "He reminded them of previous nations who rejected their prophets and were destroyed. The people of Noah were drowned in the flood, the 'Ad of Hud were destroyed by a violent wind, and the Thamud of Salih were killed by a mighty blast. These were not distant legends but historical warnings. Each nation thought they could oppose Allah's messenger without consequence, and each was annihilated.",
  "And the people of Lot are not far from you.": "Most strikingly, he mentioned the people of Lot—their neighbors in time and place. The destruction of Sodom and Gomorrah had happened recently, and the ruins were nearby. The people of Midian could not claim ignorance of what happened to those who persisted in sin. This was a fresh, local warning that should have terrified them into compliance."
},
90: {
  "So seek your Lord's forgiveness and turn to Him in repentance.": "After the warning came the solution: seek forgiveness (istighfār) and repent (tawbah). No matter what they had done, the door of mercy was still open. They could still save themselves by turning to Allah. This combination of seeking forgiveness for past sins and committing to change for the future is the essence of repentance in Islam.",
  "Surely my Lord is Most Merciful, All-Loving.\"": "Shu'aib concluded with two beautiful names of Allah: Raḥīm (Most Merciful) and Wadūd (All-Loving). Despite all His warnings of punishment, Allah's primary attributes are mercy and love. He wants people to return to Him and be saved. The name 'Wadūd' is especially tender—it speaks of genuine love and affection from Allah toward His servants. Even these stubborn fraudsters could receive Allah's love if they repented. This ending offered hope and invitation alongside the warning."
},
91: {
  "They threatened, \"O Shu'aib! We do not comprehend much of what you say,": "Instead of accepting Shu'aib's kind invitation, they responded with threats. They claimed not to understand his message—a lie that exposed their willful rejection rather than genuine confusion. His message was clear; they simply did not want to accept it. Claiming not to understand is a common excuse to avoid accountability.",
  "and surely we see you powerless among us.": "They pointed out his lack of physical power and social influence. He was 'ḍa'īf' (weak) among them—he could not force them to change or defend himself against them. This was an intimidation tactic, reminding him of their power over him. Bullies always emphasize their physical strength when they cannot defeat arguments.",
  "Were it not for your clan, we would have certainly stoned you,": "They explicitly threatened to stone him to death. The only thing restraining them was his family connection—his clan (rahṭ) would demand retribution if he was killed. Without this tribal protection, they would have murdered him already. This reveals their murderous hearts; only fear of consequences, not morality, restrained them from killing a prophet.",
  "for you are nothing to us.\"": "They concluded by declaring that Shu'aib himself meant nothing to them. His person, his words, his prophethood—all worthless in their eyes. Only his tribal connections gave him any value. This insult was meant to humiliate him and show their utter contempt for his message. How wrong they were—he was everything, as a messenger of Allah, while they were heading toward annihilation."
},
92: {
  "He said, \"O my people! Do you have more regard for my clan than for Allah,": "Shu'aib exposed the absurdity of their position. They feared his tribe but not Allah. His human family was more of a deterrent to them than the Lord of the Universe. This showed how inverted their priorities were. They respected human social structures while ignoring divine authority. True wisdom is to fear Allah above all else.",
  "turning your back on Him entirely?": "They had cast Allah behind their backs ('iṭṭakhadhtumūhu warā'akum ẓihriyyā'), meaning they had completely forgotten Him, disregarded Him, and treated Him as unimportant. While they calculated the social consequences of harming Shu'aib, they gave no thought to the divine consequences of disobeying Allah. This phrase powerfully describes how people ignore Allah while carefully navigating human relationships.",
  "Surely my Lord is Fully Aware of what you do.": "Despite their disregard for Allah, He was fully aware (muḥīṭ) of everything they did. His knowledge surrounded them completely. Every fraudulent transaction, every threat, every mocking word was recorded. They could hide nothing from Him. This was both a warning and a comfort—a warning to them that nothing escaped Allah's notice, and a comfort to Shu'aib that Allah saw their injustice toward him."
},
93: {
  "O my people! Persist in your ways, for I too will persist in mine.": "This was a final declaration of separation. Shu'aib told them to continue in their position while he continued in his. This was not giving up on them but acknowledging that he had done all he could. The choices were clear, and each side would face the consequences of their path. The phrase 'i'malū 'alā makānatikum' carries a tone of warning: continue, and see what happens.",
  "You will soon come to know who will be visited by a humiliating torment and is a liar!": "Very soon, the truth would become clear. Either Shu'aib was lying and they were right, or he was truthful and they were heading toward destruction. The humiliating punishment ('adhāb yukhzīhi) would expose the real liars. Time would tell, and time was running short.",
  "And watch! I too am watching with you!\"": "Shu'aib concluded with a challenge: let us both wait and see. He was confident in the outcome because he knew he was on the truth. The word 'irtaqibū' (watch, wait) placed them on equal footing—both parties would witness the result. But Shu'aib knew who would be vindicated and who would be destroyed."
},
94: {
  "When Our command came,": "As with the previous nations, when Allah's decree for destruction was issued, it was executed without delay. The phrase signals the end of the opportunity for repentance and the beginning of punishment.",
  "We saved Shu'aib and those who believed with him by a mercy from Us.": "Before the punishment struck, Allah evacuated the believers. Shu'aib and his followers were saved purely by Allah's mercy (raḥmah). They could not have saved themselves; their salvation was a divine gift. This pattern repeats throughout these stories: the minority who believe are preserved while the majority who reject are destroyed.",
  "And the mighty blast overtook the wrongdoers,": "The punishment came as a 'ṣayḥah'—a mighty scream or blast that killed them instantly. This was similar to what struck Thamud. The overwhelming sound destroyed them where they stood. All their threats against Shu'aib came to nothing against the sound of divine anger.",
  "so they fell lifeless in their homes,": "They collapsed dead in their very dwellings. The homes they were so proud of, where they counted their fraudulent gains, became their graves. 'Jāthimīn' describes bodies fallen forward on their faces—a posture of utter defeat and humiliation."
},
95: {
  "as if they had never lived there.": "After the punishment, it was as if the people of Midian had never existed. Their bustling markets, their grand homes, their confident threats—all erased as if they were never there. This is the fate of those who oppose Allah: total obliteration, no legacy, no memory except as a warning to others.",
  "So away with Midian as it was with Thamûd!": "The same condemnation pronounced on 'Ad and Thamud was pronounced on Midian: 'bu'dan'—away with them, cast out of mercy, destroyed and forgotten. Their fate was identical to Thamud's because their crimes were similar: rejection of the prophet and persistence in sin. Different times, different peoples, same rebellion, same end."
},
96: {
  "Indeed, We sent Moses with Our signs": "The narrative briefly mentions Musa (Moses), one of the greatest prophets. Allah sent him with 'āyāt'—signs, miracles, and proofs. These included the staff that became a serpent, his hand that shone white, and the various plagues sent upon Egypt. Musa's mission was comprehensive and his signs numerous.",
  "and compelling proof": "'Sulṭān mubīn' means clear authority or compelling proof that leaves no room for reasonable doubt. Musa came not just with miracles but with irrefutable evidence of his prophethood and the truth of his message. His proof was overwhelming, yet still Pharaoh rejected it—showing that disbelief is often willful, not intellectual."
},
97: {
  "to Pharaoh and his chiefs,": "Musa was sent specifically to Pharaoh (Fir'awn) and his ruling elite (mala'). These were the powerful decision-makers of Egypt. The message was delivered at the highest levels of society, giving them no excuse of ignorance. Pharaoh and his chiefs had direct access to a prophet of Allah.",
  "but they followed the command of Pharaoh,": "Despite the clear proofs, the chiefs followed Pharaoh rather than Musa. They chose obedience to a human tyrant over obedience to Allah. This was the fundamental error of those who follow corrupt leaders against divine guidance. Peer pressure, social position, and fear of the powerful led them to reject truth.",
  "and Pharaoh's command was not well guided.": "'Mā amru Fir'awna bi-rashīd'—Pharaoh's command, his leadership, his way, was not rightly guided. Despite his claims to divinity and wisdom, Pharaoh was leading his people toward destruction. Following misguided leaders leads to ruin, no matter how powerful those leaders seem. True guidance comes only from Allah."
},
98: {
  "He will be before his people on the Day of Judgment": "Pharaoh will lead his followers on the Day of Judgment, just as he led them in this world. 'Yaqdumu qawmahu'—he will be at the front, heading the procession. But this leadership will be toward destruction, not glory.",
  "and will lead them into the Fire.": "His leadership ends at the gates of Hell. He will lead his followers (those who obeyed him over Allah) directly into the Fire. The same people who followed his commands in this world will follow him into eternal punishment. Bad leadership has eternal consequences.",
  "What an evil place to be led into!": "'Bi'sa al-wirdu al-mawrūd'—what a terrible destination to arrive at! The 'wird' is a watering place where animals come to drink. But this 'watering place' is the Fire. The imagery is grimly ironic: they came seeking refreshment and found only burning punishment. Those who follow tyrants think they are headed somewhere good; they end up in the worst possible destination."
},
99: {
  "They were followed by a curse in this life": "Pharaoh and his followers were cursed in this worldly life. They are remembered with disgrace; their names are synonymous with tyranny and opposition to God. The civilization they built ended in ignominy, and their mummy (in the case of the Pharaoh of the Exodus) remains as a sign of their humiliation.",
  "and will receive another on the Day of Judgment.": "A second curse awaits them on the Day of Resurrection. They will be publicly condemned before all of creation and then cast into Hell. The curse is doubled—disgrace in this world and disgrace in the next.",
  "What an evil gift to receive!": "'Bi'sa al-rifdu al-marfūd'—what a terrible gift they received! The 'rifd' is a gift or support. Normally, gifts are welcome, but this 'gift' is a curse and punishment. The people who hoped for rewards found only ruin. This is the ironic 'gift' given to those who follow corrupt leaders and oppose prophets."
},
100: {
  "These are accounts, We relate to you O Prophet, of the destroyed cities.": "Allah addresses Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him), explaining that these stories of destroyed nations are related for a purpose. They are not mere entertainment but contain lessons and warnings. The cities of 'Ad, Thamud, Lot, Midian, and Pharaoh's Egypt were all real places with real histories.",
  "Some are still standing barren, while others have been mowed down.": "Some ruins of these cities remain visible ('qā'im'), serving as physical reminders of divine punishment. Others have been completely destroyed ('ḥaṣīd'), leveled like harvested crops with no trace remaining. The Quraysh of Mecca were familiar with these ruins from their trade journeys. The remains stand as warnings; the completely vanished show that nothing lasts when Allah decrees destruction."
},
101: {
  "We did not wrong them, rather they wronged themselves.": "Allah emphatically states that He did not treat these nations unjustly. Every punishment was deserved. They wronged themselves through their disbelief, their rejection of prophets, and their persistence in sin. Allah does not punish without cause; every destroyed nation brought destruction upon themselves.",
  "The gods they invoked beside Allah were of no help at all": "When Allah's punishment came, where were the idols they worshipped? Where were the false gods they trusted? They could do nothing—not because they chose not to help, but because they had no power at all. The people had devoted their worship to entities that were completely helpless.",
  "when the command of your Lord came,": "When Allah's decree was issued, no power in the universe could prevent it. Not the mighty 'Ad with their strength, not Thamud with their mountain fortresses, not the wealthy people of Midian, not Pharaoh with his armies. When Allah decides, nothing can resist.",
  "and only contributed to their ruin.": "Not only were the false gods useless for help, but they actually increased the destruction (tatbīb) of their worshippers. By diverting people from the true God, they ensured that their worshippers would earn Allah's wrath. The idols contributed to their doom rather than their salvation. This is the reality of shirk—it leads only to loss."
},
102: {
  "Such is the crushing grip of your Lord": "This is how Allah seizes nations that persist in wrongdoing. His 'akhdh' (grip, seizure) is powerful and inescapable. When He takes hold of a people, nothing can rescue them. The word emphasizes both the action of seizing and the firmness of the hold.",
  "when He seizes the societies entrenched in wrongdoing.": "The punishment is specifically for societies ('qurā') that are 'ẓālimah'—steeped in wrongdoing, established in injustice. These are not communities that make occasional mistakes but those that have institutionalized oppression and sin. When wrongdoing becomes systemic and persistent, divine seizure becomes inevitable.",
  "Indeed, His grip is terribly painful and severe.": "'Alīm' (painful) and 'shadīd' (severe, intense) describe the punishment. It is not gentle or moderate but intense suffering. This description should strike fear into the hearts of any community following a similar path of institutionalized injustice and rejection of divine guidance. The pattern established with previous nations continues—and the grip remains just as painful and severe."
},
103: {
  "Surely in this is a sign for those who fear the torment of the Hereafter.": "The stories of the destroyed nations are not just historical accounts but signs (āyāt) for those who reflect. However, only those who fear the punishment of the Hereafter will truly benefit from these signs. A person who does not believe in accountability after death will dismiss these stories as ancient tales. But one who fears meeting Allah will see in these accounts a clear warning: the same God who destroyed 'Ad, Thamud, and Pharaoh will judge all of humanity. These worldly punishments are previews of the greater punishment awaiting the disbelievers in the Hereafter.",
  "That is a Day for which humanity will be gathered": "The Day of Judgment is described as 'yawm majmū' lahu al-nās'—a day for which all humanity will be assembled. Every human who ever lived, from Adam to the last person, will be gathered in one place. No one will be absent, no one can hide, and no one can escape. This gathering is the ultimate assembly, making all worldly gatherings seem insignificant. The purpose of this gathering is judgment—each person will face their record.",
  "and a Day that will be witnessed by all.": "'Yawm mashhūd'—a day that is witnessed. All of creation will witness this Day: the angels, the prophets, the believers, the disbelievers, the jinn—everyone will be present and watching. Nothing will be hidden; all deeds will be exposed. This public nature of the Day adds to its gravity. Sins committed in private will be revealed, and righteousness done secretly will be made manifest. It is also a day witnessed because it has been foretold and awaited throughout history."
},
104: {
  "We only delay it for a fixed term.": "Some might wonder why the Day of Judgment has not yet come if Allah's punishment is certain. This verse answers: it is only delayed until an appointed time (ajal ma'dūd). Allah has decreed a specific term for this world to continue, and when that term expires, the Day will come. The delay is not due to any inability on Allah's part but is according to His perfect wisdom and timing. Every day that passes brings the Hour closer. The delay gives people time to repent, but it does not mean the Day will never come. The countdown is in motion; the question is not 'if' but 'when.'"
},
105: {
  "When that Day arrives, no one will dare speak except with His permission.": "On that Day, Allah's authority will be absolute and manifest. No one will speak without His permission (illā bi-idhnihi). The arrogant who spoke freely against Allah and His messengers will be silenced. The mighty who commanded and threatened will be mute. Only when Allah permits will anyone utter a word. This shows the complete reversal of worldly dynamics—those who silenced truth in this world will be silenced on that Day. Even the prophets will only speak when granted permission. This verse creates a scene of utter submission before Allah's majesty.",
  "Some of them will be miserable, others joyful.": "Humanity will be divided into two ultimate categories: the 'shaqī' (miserable, wretched) and the 'sa'īd' (happy, joyful). There is no third category, no neutral position. Every person will either achieve eternal joy or eternal misery based on their faith and deeds. This simple division cuts through all the complexities of worldly status—rich and poor, powerful and weak, famous and unknown—all reduced to two outcomes. The question each person should ask is: which group will I be in?"
},
106: {
  "As for those bound for misery, they will be in the Fire,": "The miserable ones (al-ladhīna shaqū) will be in the Hellfire. Their destination is certain and unavoidable. This is the consequence of disbelief and persistence in sin. The Fire is not metaphorical but a real place of punishment prepared for those who rejected guidance. Their misery is complete—they chose wretchedness by their own actions.",
  "where they will be sighing and gasping,": "The inhabitants of Hell will make sounds of extreme suffering: 'zafīr' (sighing, exhaling in distress) and 'shahīq' (gasping, inhaling with difficulty). These are the sounds of someone in unbearable agony—breathing becomes a torment. The Fire is so intense that even the basic act of breathing becomes painful. These sounds will fill Hell, a constant reminder of the consequences of rejecting Allah. The imagery is vivid and terrifying, meant to warn people while they still have time to change."
},
107: {
  "staying there forever, as long as the heavens and the earth will endure,": "The people of Hell will remain there eternally (khālidīna fīhā). The phrase 'as long as the heavens and earth endure' was an Arabic expression meaning 'forever' since the Arabs considered the heavens and earth permanent. In the Hereafter, there will be new heavens and earth, and the punishment will continue in that existence as well. The permanence of Hell for disbelievers is affirmed clearly—there is no end to their torment.",
  "except what your Lord wills.": "This exception (illā mā shā'a Rabbuk) affirms Allah's absolute sovereignty. Nothing restricts Allah's will—not even statements about eternity. Some scholars understand this as referring to the sinful believers who will eventually be removed from Hell after purification. Others see it as emphasizing that everything, including the duration of Hell, is under Allah's control. Allah is not bound by anything; His will is supreme. This exception does not mean Hell is not eternal for disbelievers—other verses make that clear—but it maintains that Allah's decree is always the final word.",
  "Surely your Lord does what He intends.": "'Inna Rabbaka fa''ālun limā yurīd'—Your Lord does whatever He wills. This is a powerful statement of Allah's absolute power and freedom. He is not questioned about what He does (21:23). His actions are always based on perfect wisdom, even when we cannot understand them. This phrase should inspire both awe and submission—awe at His unlimited power and submission to His wise decrees."
},
108: {
  "And as for those destined to joy, they will be in Paradise,": "In contrast to the miserable, the joyful ones (al-ladhīna su'idū) will be in Paradise (Jannah). Their joy is not temporary but eternal. They achieved what truly matters—Allah's pleasure and eternal bliss. All the struggles of this world fade away in the face of this permanent happiness. They will have everything they desire and more than they could ever imagine.",
  "staying there forever, as long as the heavens and the earth will endure,": "Just as the people of Hell remain forever in their punishment, the people of Paradise remain forever in their bliss. The parallel structure emphasizes that both outcomes are eternal. However, the nature of that eternity is vastly different—one of torment, one of delight.",
  "except what your Lord wills—": "Again, Allah's will is mentioned as supreme. For the people of Paradise, this exception might refer to the time before they enter Jannah (the period of judgment) or simply reaffirms that all matters are under Allah's control. The people of Paradise do not need to fear this exception—their reward is guaranteed and will not be diminished.",
  "a generous giving, without end.": "'Aṭā'an ghayra majdhūdh'—a gift that is never cut off. Unlike worldly blessings that end, the gifts of Paradise continue forever without interruption or reduction. This phrase provides additional assurance to the believers: their reward is not only eternal but continually renewed. Allah's generosity knows no bounds, and the pleasures of Paradise never diminish or cease. This is the ultimate security—permanent, abundant blessing from the Most Generous."
},
109: {
  "So do not be in doubt O Prophet about what those pagans worship.": "Allah commands the Prophet (and by extension all believers) not to have any doubt about the falsehood of idol worship. After all the stories of destroyed nations, the case against polytheism is clear. The idols have no power; they could not save their worshippers. There is no room for uncertainty—what these people worship is false, and their practice leads to destruction.",
  "They worship nothing except what their forefathers worshipped before them.": "The pagans of Mecca had no independent reason for their idol worship. They simply followed their ancestors blindly. This is a recurring theme—people cling to traditions without examining whether those traditions are true. Their worship was not based on revelation, reason, or evidence, but merely on inherited custom. Such blind following leads to the same fate as those ancestors who also rejected prophets.",
  "And We will certainly give them their share of punishment in full, without any reduction.": "Allah promises to give them their full portion (naṣīb) of punishment without any decrease (ghayra manqūṣ). Just as they followed their forefathers in disbelief, they will follow them in punishment. Every wrong they committed will be accounted for completely. There will be no discounts, no overlooked sins, no partial punishment. Divine justice is precise and complete."
},
110: {
  "Indeed, We had given Moses the Scripture, but differences arose regarding it.": "This verse draws a parallel between the situation of Prophet Muhammad (peace be upon him) and Prophet Musa. Allah gave Musa the Torah, but his people differed about it—some believed, some disbelieved, some distorted its meaning. The same pattern occurred with the Quran. This is not a failure of the scripture but a result of human free will and rebellion. Prophets face similar challenges across history.",
  "Had it not been for a prior decree from your Lord,": "Allah had already decreed that judgment would not be immediate but would wait until the Day of Resurrection. This 'prior decree' (kalimah sabaqat) is Allah's decision to give humanity time, to allow the test of faith to unfold, and to reserve final judgment for the Hereafter.",
  "their differences would have been settled at once.": "If not for this decree, Allah would have immediately punished the wrongdoers and vindicated the believers. The disputes about truth would have been settled instantly through divine intervention. But Allah's wisdom dictated that this world be a place of testing, not immediate retribution. The delay in punishment is mercy and opportunity, not weakness.",
  "They are truly in alarming doubt about it.": "Those who rejected the scripture remained in deep, troubling doubt (shakk murīb). Their doubt was not intellectual inquiry but willful refusal to accept truth. This doubt caused them unease, as deep down they suspected the truth but refused to embrace it. Such doubt leads to destruction because it prevents sincere seeking of guidance."
},
111: {
  "And surely your Lord will fully pay all for their deeds.": "Allah guarantees that everyone will receive the full recompense for their actions. 'Layuwaffiyannahum'—He will certainly and completely pay them. Good deeds will be fully rewarded, and evil deeds will be fully punished. Nothing is lost or overlooked. This is a promise and a warning—the righteous should be encouraged, and the wicked should be terrified.",
  "He is certainly All-Aware of what they do.": "'Innahu bimā ya'malūna khabīr'—He is completely aware of what they do. The word 'khabīr' implies deep, thorough knowledge of both external actions and internal intentions. Nothing is hidden from Allah. He knows why people do what they do, not just what they do. This comprehensive awareness ensures perfect justice—no one will be wronged, and no one will escape accountability."
},
112: {
  "So be steadfast as you are commanded O Prophet,": "'Fastaqim kamā umirta'—this is one of the most significant commands in the Quran. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said this verse and its like caused him to age (showing its weight). Istiqāmah means to be straight, firm, consistent, and unwavering on the path of truth. It means continuing on the straight path without deviation, neither adding innovations nor neglecting obligations. It requires persistence through difficulty, steadfastness in obedience, and consistency in character. This command applies to all believers—we must remain firm on the guidance we have been given.",
  "along with those who turn in submission to Allah with you.": "The command extends to all who have repented (tāba) and joined the Prophet in faith. Steadfastness is a collective responsibility. The Muslim community must support each other in remaining firm. Those who turned to Allah in repentance must continue on that path together with the Prophet and with each other.",
  "And do not transgress.": "'Walā taṭghaw'—do not exceed the limits. Even in religious zeal, there must be balance. Transgression includes extremism, adding to the religion what is not from it, excessive harshness, or going beyond what Allah has commanded. Islam is a middle path, and deviation in any direction—whether toward laxity or extremism—is transgression.",
  "Surely He is All-Seeing of what you believers do.": "Allah sees everything the believers do. This is both a warning and a comfort. A warning because even believers can slip into transgression and will be held accountable. A comfort because Allah sees every sacrifice, every struggle, every act of obedience done for His sake. Nothing goes unnoticed by the All-Seeing Lord."
},
113: {
  "And do not be inclined to the wrongdoers or you will be touched by the Fire.": "'Walā tarkanū ilā alladhīna ẓalamū'—do not lean toward, rely on, support, or show approval of wrongdoers. The word 'rukūn' implies any form of inclination, whether emotional support, cooperation, or even just admiration. The warning is severe: even inclining toward oppressors risks being touched by the Fire. This does not mean avoiding all interaction but prohibits supporting, endorsing, or approving of their wrongdoing. The believer must maintain clear distance from oppression and those who practice it.",
  "For then you would have no protectors other than Allah,": "If you incline toward wrongdoers and earn Allah's punishment, who will protect you? Your wrongdoer friends cannot help you against Allah. You would have no allies (awliyā') besides Allah, and having opposed His guidance, you would have forfeited His protection as well. This is a state of utter helplessness.",
  "nor would you be helped.": "No help would come from any quarter. Not from the wrongdoers you supported, not from your own resources, not from anyone. When Allah's punishment comes, there is no escape and no assistance. This triple warning—Fire, no protectors, no help—should make believers very careful about their associations and allegiances."
},
114: {
  "Establish prayer O Prophet at both ends of the day and in the early part of the night.": "This verse prescribes prayer times: 'ṭarafay al-nahār' (both ends of the day) refers to Fajr (morning) and Maghrib/Asr (evening) prayers, while 'zulafan min al-layl' (portions of the night) refers to Isha and possibly Tahajjud (night prayer). Prayer is the antidote to inclining toward wrongdoers—it keeps the believer connected to Allah. Establishing prayer means performing it properly, on time, with focus, and consistently. Prayer is the pillar of religion and the believer's weapon against sin.",
  "Surely good deeds wipe out evil deeds.": "'Inna al-ḥasanāti yudhhibna al-sayyi'āt'—this is a profound principle of Islamic ethics. Good deeds (especially prayer) erase minor sins. The Prophet (peace be upon him) explained this verse by saying that the five daily prayers, Friday to Friday, and Ramadan to Ramadan erase the sins between them as long as major sins are avoided (Muslim). This gives hope to sinners: sincere good deeds can compensate for slips and mistakes. However, this applies to minor sins; major sins require specific repentance.",
  "That is a reminder for the mindful.": "This principle is 'dhikrā lil-dhākirīn'—a reminder for those who remember, those who are mindful, those who pay attention. Only those who are conscious of Allah will benefit from this reminder. The heedless will ignore it. Being mindful means constantly remembering Allah, being aware of one's actions, and striving to do more good to compensate for weaknesses."
},
115: {
  "And be patient!": "'Waṣbir'—be patient. After steadfastness, prayer, and good deeds, patience is commanded. The path is difficult, and challenges are many. Patience is required to maintain steadfastness, to continue praying, to keep doing good when results are not immediate. Patience in obeying Allah, patience in avoiding sin, and patience in facing trials—all are necessary for the believer.",
  "Certainly Allah does not discount the reward of the good-doers.": "'Inna Allāha lā yuḍī'u ajra al-muḥsinīn'—Allah never wastes the reward of those who do good (muḥsinīn). Every good deed is recorded and will be rewarded. Unlike worldly investments that can be lost, investments in good deeds with Allah are completely secure. The muḥsinīn are those who strive for excellence in their worship and conduct. Their efforts are never in vain. This promise should motivate believers to persevere, knowing that nothing is lost with Allah."
},
116: {
  "If only there had been among the destroyed peoples before you, O believers,": "This verse expresses regret over a missed opportunity. Among the destroyed nations, there were very few people of virtue who actively forbade corruption. If there had been more such people, perhaps the nations could have been saved. This is a lesson for the Muslim community: the presence of people who actively oppose evil is essential for society's survival.",
  "virtuous individuals who forbade corruption in the land—": "'Ūlū baqiyyah' refers to people of remnant virtue, those with moral substance who would stand against wrong. They would 'yanhawna 'an al-fasād'—forbid corruption and evil. Every society needs such people. When they are absent or silent, corruption spreads unchecked, and divine punishment becomes inevitable. This is the Islamic concept of 'enjoining good and forbidding evil'—a communal obligation that protects society.",
  "other than the few We had saved from the torment.": "There were some who forbade evil—the believers who followed the prophets—and these were the few who were saved. But they were too few to change the overall direction of their societies. Their virtue saved themselves but could not avert the punishment from their nations. Numbers matter; a critical mass of righteous people is needed to transform society.",
  "But the wrongdoers only pursued their worldly pleasures, becoming wicked.": "The wrongdoers (alladhīna ẓalamū) were preoccupied with 'mā utrifū fīhi'—the luxuries and pleasures they were given. They followed their desires rather than divine guidance. Their comfort made them heedless, and their wealth made them arrogant. They became 'mujrimīn'—criminals deserving punishment. This is a warning about the danger of wealth and comfort when they distract from religious obligations and moral responsibility."
},
117: {
  "And your Lord O Prophet would never destroy a society unjustly": "'Mā kāna Rabbuka liyuhlika al-qurā bi-ẓulm'—Allah would never destroy cities through injustice. Allah is absolutely just and never punishes without cause. If a society is destroyed, it is because they deserved it through their own actions. The word 'bi-ẓulm' can also be understood as 'while they are being righteous'—meaning Allah does not destroy people who are doing right.",
  "while its people were acting rightly.": "'Wa ahluhā muṣliḥūn'—while its people were reformers, doers of good, acting righteously. As long as people are striving to be good and to improve their society, Allah does not destroy them. Destruction comes when corruption becomes widespread and unchallenged. This gives communities a clear standard: be a society of muṣliḥūn (reformers and righteous people), and you will be safe from divine punishment. Allow corruption to dominate, and destruction becomes possible."
},
118: {
  "Had your Lord so willed, He would have certainly made humanity one single community of believers,": "If Allah wanted, He could have made all humanity believe. He has the power to guide every heart and leave no disbelievers. But this was not His plan. The diversity of belief is not due to Allah's inability but to His wisdom. He chose to create humans with free will, allowing them to make genuine choices about faith.",
  "but they will always choose to differ—": "'Walā yazālūna mukhtalifīn'—they will continue to differ, to disagree, to diverge in their beliefs and choices. This is the nature of humanity when given free will. Differences of opinion, belief, and practice are part of the human condition. The test of life requires this diversity; without the option to disbelieve, belief would be meaningless."
},
119: {
  "except those shown mercy by your Lord—": "Among the differing masses, there are those upon whom Allah has bestowed His mercy. These are the believers who, through Allah's grace, found guidance and held onto it. Their unity is in faith, even if they differ in other matters. Allah's mercy is what distinguishes them from the rest of humanity.",
  "and so He created them to choose freely.": "'Wa li-dhālika khalaqahum'—this is the reason for creation. Allah created humans with the capacity to choose, knowing they would differ. This choice is fundamental to the test of life. Without it, there would be no merit in faith and no justice in punishment. Allah created beings capable of choosing Him or rejecting Him, and this capacity defines human existence.",
  "And so the Word of your Lord will be fulfilled:": "Allah made a declaration that must come to pass. His words are never broken. What He decrees will certainly happen.",
  "\"I will surely fill up Hell with jinn and humans all together.\"": "This is the decree: Hell will be filled with those from both jinn and humans who chose disbelief. This statement is not a wish or a desire but a prediction based on Allah's knowledge. He knows that many will choose wrongly and will end up deserving Hell. This is not injustice—each person who enters Hell earned their place through their own choices. Allah offered guidance, sent messengers, gave warnings, but many still chose rebellion. The filling of Hell is the natural consequence of free will misused."
},
120: {
  "And We relate to you O Prophet the stories of the messengers to reassure your heart.": "'Wa kullan naquṣṣu 'alayka min anbā' al-rusul mā nuthabbittu bihi fu'ādak'—Allah tells the Prophet these stories for a purpose: to strengthen and reassure his heart. Prophethood is difficult; facing rejection, persecution, and opposition is exhausting. By knowing that previous messengers faced similar struggles and ultimately triumphed, the Prophet's heart is comforted. He is not alone in his experience; he follows a noble tradition of perseverance. This applies to all believers who face hardship for their faith.",
  "And there has come to you in this sûrah the truth,": "This surah contains al-ḥaqq—the truth. The stories are not fiction but true accounts of what happened. The theology is not invention but divine revelation. Truth has arrived, and it demands a response.",
  "a warning to the disbelievers,": "'Maw'iẓah'—a warning, an admonition for those who disbelieve. If they continue on their path, they will face what previous nations faced. The stories should shake them from their complacency and make them reconsider their rejection of the Prophet.",
  "and a reminder to the believers.": "'Dhikrā lil-mu'minīn'—a reminder for those who believe. Believers need reminders to stay firm, to maintain their faith, and to appreciate the blessings of guidance. These stories remind them of Allah's support for the righteous and His punishment of the wicked, strengthening their resolve."
},
121: {
  "Say to those who disbelieve, \"Persist in your ways;": "The Prophet is commanded to deliver a final message to the disbelievers: continue on your path (i'malū 'alā makānatikum). This is not encouragement but a declaration of separation. Each party will proceed as they choose. It carries an undertone of warning—continue, and face the consequences.",
  "we will certainly persist in ours.": "'Innā 'āmilūn'—we too will continue on our path. The Prophet and the believers will not compromise or waver. They will persist in worship, obedience, and calling to truth. The challenge is set: let each side continue, and let time reveal who is right."
},
122: {
  "And wait! Surely we too are waiting.\"": "'Wa intaẓirū innā muntaẓirūn'—wait and see what happens; we too are waiting. Both parties are waiting for the outcome. The disbelievers wait, perhaps thinking they will triumph. The believers wait, confident in Allah's promise. Time will tell—either in this world through events or in the Hereafter through judgment—who was on the truth. This mutual waiting is a challenge and a threat: the wrongdoers will eventually see their mistake, whether they like it or not."
},
123: {
  "To Allah alone belongs the knowledge of what is hidden in the heavens and the earth.": "'Lillāhi ghaybu al-samāwāti wa al-arḍ'—all unseen matters in the heavens and earth belong exclusively to Allah's knowledge. He alone knows the future, the hidden intentions of hearts, and everything concealed from creation. No one shares this absolute knowledge. When the Hour will come, what each person hides, what will happen tomorrow—all is known only to Allah.",
  "And to Him all matters are returned.": "'Wa ilayhi yurja'u al-amru kulluh'—every affair returns to Allah for judgment. All matters, whether personal or cosmic, are ultimately under His authority. Every dispute will be settled by Him, every deed judged by Him. There is no appeal beyond Allah; His decision is final.",
  "So worship Him and put your trust in Him.": "This is the conclusion and summary: 'fa'budhu wa tawakkal 'alayhi.' After all the stories, warnings, and reminders, the practical response is worship (acknowledging His lordship through obedience) and trust (relying on Him completely). These two attitudes—worship and tawakkul—encapsulate the proper relationship between the servant and Allah.",
  "And your Lord is never unaware of what you do.": "'Wa mā Rabbuka bi-ghāfilin 'ammā ta'malūn'—Allah is not heedless of your actions. Everything is observed and recorded. This final statement carries both warning and comfort: warning to the wrongdoers that their deeds are known, and comfort to the believers that their struggles and sacrifices are seen by their Lord. The surah ends as it began—with the reality of Allah's awareness and the certainty of accountability. Nothing is lost; nothing is forgotten. Every action counts, and every person will receive what they deserve."
}
}